WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.740
- Hello everyone.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.360
I am Priya Natarajan,

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.690
the current director of The Franke Program

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.000
in Science and the Humanities at Yale,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.450
which is the flagship
interdisciplinary program.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.240
I'd like to welcome you
all to the final talk

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.660
in our, Understanding the
Nature of Inference Series

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.210
that we've been hosting
at The Franke Program,

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.850
exploring inferential models, reasoning,

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.400
and experiments across disciplines.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.520
We've had a really rich series of talks,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.850
exciting talks that have
addressed issues relating to

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.400
direct causation and correlation,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.180
and how multiple modes of
inquiry help us make sense

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.030
of the world and make inferences from it.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.310
So, I invite you all to today's talk

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.817
with the intriguing title,

00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:54.750
"Inference in a Non-Conceptual World,"

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.570
by the wonderful speaker today,

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.550
Professor Brian Cantwell Smith.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.980
But before I introduce
our distinguished speaker

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.150
for this evening,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.090
I would like to first
recognize our benefactors,

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.820
Mr. And Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.030
whose generosity in funding
interdisciplinary activities

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.910
at Yale, has made all our radical

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.220
academic silo busting activities

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.680
at The Franke Program possible.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.710
I would also like to thank
the John Templeton Foundation

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.780
for funding this program of activities,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.840
including this colloquium
and discussion series.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.250
Their support has permitted
us to explore in this series

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:35.310
a novel format, a colloquium
by a distinguished speaker,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.400
followed by deep engagement
with an additional leading

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.000
thinker after the colloquium
on a separate day.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.120
In this case, tomorrow
afternoon at the same time.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.210
This unique format has
enabled serious engagement

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:52.230
while giving us some time to
reflect and digest new concepts

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.420
that we learned from
the Colloquium speaker,

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.990
while also exposing us to
highly relevant alternate

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.840
intellectual points of
view from the discussion

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.520
the following day.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.000
I am also required to
kindly remind those assemble

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.890
