00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.473
If you wish,

00:02:12.473 --> 00:02:15.810
you can submit questions
through the Q&amp;A feature.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.693
Onto today's speaker,
Professor Brian Cantwell Smith.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.410
Brian Cantwell Smith is
the Reid Hoffman Professor

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.510
of artificial intelligence and the human,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.330
as well as being Professor
of information philosophy,

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:28.800
cognitive science,

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.680
and the history and philosophy
of science and technology.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.750
And a senior fellow at Massey College

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.790
at the University of Toronto.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.980
He actually spoke to us in person

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.880
about artificial intelligence,
the challenges, the wins,

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.130
and the challenges at our program

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.140
just before the onset of the pandemic.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.620
So, we are absolutely
delighted to invite him again.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.400
He received his degrees,
all his degrees, in fact,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.060
from my favorite undergraduate
institution, MIT.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.170
So, here I have to
express my loyalty to MIT.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.810
And from '81 to '96,

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.880
he was a principal scientist at Xerox Park

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.790
and an adjunct professor
of philosophy at Stanford.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.280
He was the founder of the
Center for the Study of Language

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.040
and Information at Stanford University.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.160
Was really ahead of the
game in understanding that

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.000
information was going to
start playing an incredibly

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.090
important role in society, culture,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.950
creation of new knowledge,

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.770
and really the frontier
of technology as well.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.630
He was a founder and first president

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.480
of the Computer Professionals
for Social Responsibility

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.170
and President for the Society
of Philosophy and Psychology.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.900
And from 1996 to 2001,

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.700
he was the professor of cognitive science,

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.120
computer science and philosophy
at Indiana University.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.160
And from 2001 to 2003,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.530
he was the Kimberly Jenkins
University distinguished

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.400
professor of philosophy and
new technologies and professor

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.860
and in the departments of Philosophy

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.320
and Computer Science at Duke University.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.520
So, you get the sense
that Ryan has had his foot

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.080
in multiple camps,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.150
so multiple feet in multiple camps

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.830
throughout his academic career.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.380
So, if there is someone
whose intellectual interests

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:13.290
cannot be siloed, it is him.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.110
He's exemplar of the
interdisciplinary scholarship

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.790
that we stand for.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.640
And, Professor Smith moved
to the University of Toronto

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.510
in 2003 initially, to serve
as the Dean of the Faculty

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.840
of Information and has stayed there since.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.800
And our distinguished
conversant tomorrow afternoon

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.730
is Professor Joseph Rouse
from Wesleyan University.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.490
And his primary research
interests are in the philosophy of

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.710
science, the history of
20th century philosophy,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.840
interdisciplinary science studies,

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.000
and the Social Theory of Practices.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.790
I'll give a much more
detailed introduction to him

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.530
before our session tomorrow.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.940
Without further ado,

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.800
I turning the floor over to
Professor Brian Cantwell Smith.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.970
Welcome, Brian.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.230
It's all yours.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.180
- Well, thank you very much, Priya,

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.910
and thanks to you and to Ty and to Guy

00:05:05.551 --> 00:05:09.163
and to the Frankes and to the
Templeton Foundation and so on

00:05:10.134 --> 00:05:11.970
and so forth of all the people
for making this possible.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:12.803
And then also,

00:05:12.803 --> 00:05:14.650
thank you all of those who are attending

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:16.830
for a chance to have this conversation.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.690
Cause I really hope it will be a kind of

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:19.620
open-ended conversation.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.727
What I want to convince you of,

00:05:21.727 --> 00:05:25.500
if I have a chance is of an orientation,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.320
but not of an answer to a whole.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.870
And I think the orientation
opens up all kinds of questions

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:34.230
to which, as far as I know,
none of us yet have answers.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.080
So, that's what I hope to talk about.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.130
There are four parts to this,
or actually five in a way.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.920
I'm gonna talk about
conceptual representation first

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.010
and then the conceptual world, second.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.980
And then non-conceptual
representation, third.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.620
And the non-conceptual world, fourth.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.663
And then on apple log on explainability.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.020
I wanna make one remark before we start

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.243
about AI and contemporary AI.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.600
I won't be talking all that much about AI,

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:04.830
but some towards the end.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.440
But, one thing that's true
is I've been in and around AI

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.240
for a long time.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.100
In fact, I'm pretty ancient.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.720
I wrote my first big AI program in 1968,

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.400
and there have been a lot
of changes since then.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:18.233
Needless to say,

00:06:18.233 --> 00:06:20.550
there was the first wave enthusiasm

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.230
and then long periods of AI winter

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.420
and a lot of different weather in there.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.300
And a resurgence of
second wave AI and stuff.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.410
What's interesting to me
and sobering is that today,

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.180
so for example, when I teach classes,

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.980
I'm just finishing one up this fall.

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:40.020
The word AI, the Moniker.AI,

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:41.730
but also the word artificial intelligence,

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.620
means statistical generalizations

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:44.850
over massive amounts of data.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.253
That's what the students
just think this is.

00:06:48.433 --> 00:06:50.010
A particular kind of data too,

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.380
corpuscular non-narrative data,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.420
the sort of data that
can go into databases

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.493
or else decontextualize text strings.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.790
And as I've argued in a recent book,

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.870
there's no real serious
attempt to get these systems

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.150
to understand what they're talking about.

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:06.983
And I think,

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.470
there's sort of pure,

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:11.920
there's a wonderful phrase of

00:07:15.442 --> 00:07:17.820
Timnit Gebru and others

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.340
called stochastical parrots.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.160
That's how they characterize these system,

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:22.993
as stochastical parrots.

00:07:22.993 --> 00:07:25.410
And I think there's a
lot of truth to that.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:26.940
I don't think there's
any real semantics to

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.257
these today's systems.

00:07:28.257 --> 00:07:30.090
And some people think
that with enough syntax

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:30.923
you get semantics,

00:07:30.923 --> 00:07:34.350
which I think is about as
wrong as thinking that if you

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.200
travel fast enough in strange
loops on two dimensions,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.660
you're gonna end up in a third dimension.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:42.870
So, I think AI at the moment
is in a very particular

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:45.630
and rather narrow conception.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.000
That's not how it started.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.803
When I was in MIT in the seventies,

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.580
you know, a lot of people
were inspired by the golem,

00:07:56.052 --> 00:07:58.590
the myth from Jewish mystical history

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:05.160
in a way that the idea
was that man was created

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.430
in God's image, was a recursive plane.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.350
Because if man was in
creating God's image,

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.780
then that meant man could
create in God's image

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:13.860
as well and stuff.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.360
A lot of the early pioneers of AI at MIT,

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.660
like Marvin Minsky, one of
the founders of the field,

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:23.760
Gerald Sussman, Joel Moses,

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.190
who went on to be president
of the university,

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:29.220
admitted not necessarily publicly
that they'd been inspired

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.070
by the golem myth as children.

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:33.618
And the idea was that we would
understand understanding.

00:08:33.618 --> 00:08:35.730
We would understand the human condition.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:38.607
We would understand our place
in the universe and how--

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.000
Where we came from and so on and so forth.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.650
Marvin Minsky proposed we buy a church

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:45.250
and put a computer on the altar.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.280
Now, that was a little imperialist,
but there was, I think,

00:08:53.459 --> 00:08:55.710
a grandeur to that original vision.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:58.080
Which I think has been lost.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:58.913
And I think,

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:03.120
I think the aim of this
talk is not to indict

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:04.140
current models of AI

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:07.110
But, when I talk about AI,

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.060
you should know that I
come from that tradition,

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:11.760
and I'm not referring to
the current narrow view.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.510
I'm actually referring to
things that might allow us

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:17.190
to move from the current narrow view

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.703
to a much bigger, broader,
maybe more inspiring vision.

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:22.643
Okay.

00:09:22.643 --> 00:09:24.423
Anyway, part one,
conceptual representation.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.130
So, when philosophers
particularly talk about

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.380
the content of a representation,

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.410
they refer to something like what it means

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:37.380
or how it presents the world as being.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:38.890
So, the content might be that

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.170
thanks for driving up in
front of the Supreme Court,

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:44.370
or god knows what it is.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.790
We could spend a semester
on the relationship between

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:54.060
what something means and how
it presents the world as being.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:55.060
But I don't need to,

00:09:55.981 --> 00:09:57.270
I don't need to do into that.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:58.290
I just think for the moment,

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:02.310
a sort of informal notion
of meaning will do.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.850
In 1982, Gareth Evans,
a philosopher at Oxford,

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.970
introduced the notion of
non-conceptual content.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:10.140
Now, non-conceptual content,

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:11.130
if anything is obvious,

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:12.690
it's defined in opposition to the notion

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.010
of conceptual content.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:15.510
And so the question was what intent,

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.220
conceptual content was.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.910
And that kind of triggered a whole debate.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:22.560
Basically, I'm gonna talk about it more,

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.680
but conceptual content was
the kind of content that

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:27.810
everybody assumed up until then.

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:31.170
And underlies every form
of logic that you have ever

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:33.540
imagined, including fuzzy logic.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.070
I mean, it's just the sort of model,

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.293
on which all these
formal systems are based.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:42.113
Okay.

00:10:42.113 --> 00:10:43.260
What is it in particular?

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:44.283
Conceptual content.

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.673
Very roughly, a conceptual representation

00:10:47.673 --> 00:10:50.100
is a representation that
represents the world in terms of

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:51.779
objects, properties, relations, sets,

00:10:51.779 --> 00:10:54.033
attributes, and so on and so forth.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:58.830
As in logic, as in model
theory, as in classical AI,

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:00.933
GOFI, as John Hogan called it.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:03.900
As in XML and so on and so forth.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:07.260
It's just an extraordinarily
widespread view

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:10.353
about how the world is and
what it is to represent it.

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:14.283
What else, in fact, could it be?

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:17.883
So, we'll get to that in a minute.

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:21.750
But, I wanna say something
about how conceptual content

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:23.520
has been theorized.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:27.480
Two philosophers in particular
have written in deep ways

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.463
about the notion of conceptual content,

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:34.470
people of enormous intelligence.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:35.760
Gareth Evans, who I mentioned,

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.440
who introduced the notion
of non-conceptual content.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.690
He was an enfant-terrible at Oxford.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:43.950
He died at 34, I think

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:45.650
having done a huge amount of work.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.080
His idea of conceptual
content was a representation

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.700
that's algebraically structured.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:53.400
So, I'm just gonna share the
screen here for a second.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.970
He articulated what he called
a generality condition.

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:04.970
Basically, a representation is
conceptual if it says A is F,

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:07.930
you know, that somebody is tall or

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:09.983
you know...

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.710
The storm is gonna be intense
or something like that.

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:19.470
It's conceptual only if the
system that's representing that

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:22.170
is capable of entertaining
the thought or representing

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:23.003
or whatever.

00:12:23.003 --> 00:12:25.920
Not only that A is F, but also that B is F

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.330
and C is F and so on and so forth

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.400
for any other B and c of
which it has a conception.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.670
And also, that A is G and A
is H and so on for any A, G,

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.710
and H of when it has Conception.

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.050
Modulo various appropriateness conditions.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:39.270
So, you know,

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:42.210
beans or green might be
okay, dreams are green.

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:44.130
Famous sentence of Chowski's.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.900
Doesn't quite fit because
dreams don't have color.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:47.040
So, that's an appropriate condition.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.773
But the logical structure takes that form.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:54.630
Note that this generality
condition is not framed

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:56.430
in terms of syntax,

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.520
but it lends itself to
being read syntactically,

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:00.353
which is to say,

00:13:00.353 --> 00:13:04.650
if a representation has the
form of A is F or F of A,

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:08.670
syntactically, then that system
is capable of writing down

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:13.670
G of A, or H of A, or H of B, or whatever.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:19.560
Since contemporary AI reads
everything, syntactically,

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:21.450
in fact it's defined all kinds of words,

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:24.030
including semantics to be words of syntax.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.330
That's a discussion point.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:31.370
This conception of
conceptuality is very...

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:36.990
Fits swimmingly with
current conceptions of AI

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:38.430
and so on and so forth.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:41.190
So, a lot of people in
AI just think that this,

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:42.840
that Evan's generality condition

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.690
applies to their structures directly.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:46.950
John McDowell,

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:48.420
perhaps one of the most
important philosophers

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:49.803
in the last 50 years,

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:55.260
has a different there
tradition of being conceptual.

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:58.080
He says that it
representation is conceptual.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.783
If it transparency represents
the world as it is,

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:05.400
ie, it fits into a
judgment about the world.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.990
Not a complete judgment about the world

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:10.170
because there's no such
judgment that would be too big,

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:12.360
but a judgment about a complete world

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:14.260
in the sense of world that's at whole.

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:17.220
On the face of it,

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:19.290
McDowell's characterization
is much less syntactic.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:21.090
It doesn't have to do with the structure

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:23.253
of the representation itself.

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:25.470
And also, to be honest,

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:27.780
I don't think McDowell's
characterization is anything that

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:31.950
anyone in AI or that any system
in AI is capable of meeting

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:33.800
because it has to be about the world.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:38.010
But, they're not exactly the same.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.380
So, let's turn to part three,

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:41.340
sorry, part two,

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.650
about the conceptual world.

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:46.740
Why are these two
characterizations of conceptual

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:49.980
thought to be sort of the same or at least

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:51.680
comparable or at least compatible?

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:54.420
The reason they're viewed as the same,

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:55.830
I think by most people,

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:57.840
and in fact a lot of
people don't distinguish

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:00.300
between these two characterizations.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.040
I think the reason they're
thought to be compatible

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:03.990
is because of an ontological assumption

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:06.603
that underlies people's
understandings of them.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.320
And a chronological
assumption being an assumption

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:11.430
about the structure of the world,

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:13.740
not the structure of
representation or thought,

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:15.640
but the structure of the world itself.

00:15:17.351 --> 00:15:20.730
And that basically,

00:15:20.730 --> 00:15:22.350
representations that are structured

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:25.500
according to this generality
condition of Evans represent

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:27.303
the world as it really is.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.920
So, I'm gonna extend ordinary
usage and use the word

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.770
conceptual, not just for representations,

00:15:34.770 --> 00:15:37.173
but for the world thereby represented.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:41.280
And I will say that a conceptual
world is the familiar world

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.250
in a way to our imaginations
of objects, properties,

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:47.910
relations sets and stuff that
the world itself consists of.

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:50.400
Objects, exemplifying properties,
standing in relations,

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.170
grouped together in sets,

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:54.390
exemplifying properties with attributes

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:55.583
and so on and so forth.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:00.053
So...

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:06.150
So, setting Evans and
McDowell's definitions aside,

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:09.450
I'm gonna actually take
conceptual in my usage,

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:12.570
as any content that has
this ontological character.

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.570
Any content that represents
the world in terms of

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:18.203
object properties, relations,
and so on and so forth.

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:20.573
Now...

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.160
This assumption that that's
what the world likes,

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.350
that's what the world is like,

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.753
is extraordinarily widespread.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:36.300
And it's so widespread
that in fact it's difficult

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.790
with students to actually motivate them

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:40.380
or enable them or something,

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.210
lead them to believe that anything else

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:43.473
might even be possible.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:48.930
I wanna point to a wonderful
paper of Jeffrey Nunberg,

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.540
who in fact passed away just recently,

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:53.100
on the rise of information.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:54.330
Jeffrey's an extremely smart guy.

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.370
He's such a smart guy that it's
actually sometimes difficult

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:59.850
to figure out that his papers
are as profound as they are.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:00.683
But he wrote a paper called,

00:17:00.683 --> 00:17:02.490
"Farewell to the Information Age",

00:17:02.490 --> 00:17:05.310
which, but if we don't get to
the farewell as first of all,

00:17:05.310 --> 00:17:08.490
he starts about actually when
did the information arise?

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:11.340
He's tracing the notion of information.

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:16.340
When did it actually
enter our conceptions?

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:18.510
Priya pointed out that
information is a hugely

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:19.710
important notion.

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:23.010
Now, underlies all kinds
of technical projects,

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:24.010
scientific projects.

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:25.770
But, he wants to know sort of

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:27.723
when was it invented, as it were?

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:31.800
And he goes back to the--

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:34.590
Through history, to the
rise of print, basically,

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:39.590
when print became in fact
common form of public discourse.

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:44.750
And, his sense is that
print led to the idea

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:51.320
of snippets of text being
extractable and recombinable

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.840
in ways that actually could then convey

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:55.830
content about the world.

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:58.233
Like the temperature is 96 degrees or,

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:03.050
you know, pine trees are
green or whatever it might be.

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:07.230
And his characterization of
information is that it was the

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:10.710
idea that the nature of the
world is such that it can be

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:14.730
reduced to these corpuscular
compact little bits

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:17.760
of quote on quote, information, right?

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:20.223
In fact, Jeff's such a colorful guy.

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:22.800
I once asked him,

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:24.180
I dunno if this is in print or not,

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:25.890
but it's what in fact information was.

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:27.210
And he said, "Oh, information?

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:29.640
That's what happens when you
take a book and you shake it

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:32.277
by the spine so hard that
all the words fall out."

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:35.920
And I think that's a good way to remember

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:38.700
his sense that it's,

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:43.050
it's the sort of erosion
of any sense that narrative

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:45.600
is the locus of our understanding.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.757
And instead that our
claims about the world

00:18:47.757 --> 00:18:50.220
are in fact, come in these little bits.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:52.470
There are other assumptions
underlying this notion of

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:53.303
information too,

00:18:53.303 --> 00:18:55.630
that the properties are
concepts and stuff that

00:18:56.940 --> 00:18:59.400
these bits of the world
exemplify our uniform

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:01.170
across all instances.

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:02.610
So all pine trees,

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:03.443
not that they're all the same,

00:19:03.443 --> 00:19:04.530
but in being a pine tree,

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:08.253
they're pinetreeness is
uniform across all pine trees.

00:19:11.948 --> 00:19:13.860
And so on and so forth.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:15.960
So that you could in fact have pine tree

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:20.883
as the type of a plant in a
database or something like that.

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:27.270
- Brian, I have a quick question.

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:28.103
- Yes.

00:19:28.103 --> 00:19:30.270
- But this kind of getting to the essence

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:31.710
of the pinetreeness,
- Right.

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:33.600
- Sort of the essential portfolio,

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.683
does it always imply a
reduction, reductionism?

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:39.417
- Well, it--

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:44.370
Boy, I wish I had a bottle of whiskey

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:47.567
and I could pour virtual
glasses of whiskey around.

00:19:47.567 --> 00:19:51.330
It doesn't imply reductionism.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:52.163
I mean, first of all,

00:19:52.163 --> 00:19:53.730
reductionism is not itself technically

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:55.020
particularly well defined.

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:58.020
One version of reductionism
is that there's a basic

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:01.320
foundational set of
fundamental primitives.

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:04.770
Like, physical primitives
or something like that.

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:07.600
And that you can build

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:10.380
everything up outta them.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.330
This assumption--

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:13.163
I don't necessarily
have a good name for it,

00:20:13.163 --> 00:20:16.500
but it's not that, you
might have, you know,

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:21.080
midwife in a database or you know...

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:27.603
User friendly as a
characterization of a system.

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:30.930
And you'd still think
that user friendly is a

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:33.090
sort of formal concept in
the sense that it can be

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:34.830
applied to something, without
actually thinking you can

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:37.170
reduce any of the concept to any others.

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:38.250
So, you could have gazillion,

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:40.710
you could have every word
in the dictionary in there.

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:43.050
So, it's not actually
reductionist in the classic sense.

00:20:43.050 --> 00:20:46.320
It does have a bit of character
of reduction of everything

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.960
to these corpuscular bits.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:52.050
But, not to an underlying level

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:53.820
out of which they're assembled.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:55.860
So, that's just an important distinction.

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:56.693
- Thank you.

00:20:56.693 --> 00:20:58.676
Yeah, for that clarification.

00:20:58.676 --> 00:21:02.043
- Okay, so let's turn to
non-conceptual representation.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:06.000
As I say, it was introduced
by Garreth Evans in 1982.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:09.150
He's an enfant-terrible of Oxford.

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:13.260
I actually knew him at
MIT as a graduate student

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:15.453
by coincidence and by my luck.

00:21:16.410 --> 00:21:17.460
And he strode,

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:18.750
I would meet him at two in the morning,

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:19.583
I mean, not by plan,

00:21:19.583 --> 00:21:21.480
I would just run into him at
two in the morning in Cambridge

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:23.310
and he would be striding around.

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:25.500
And he thought Cambridge, Mass.
was not a big enough place

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:27.210
to do philosophy because
there wasn't not enough place

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:29.433
for long walks in the middle of the night.

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:31.343
But anyway,

00:21:31.343 --> 00:21:34.263
he introduced the notion of
non-conceptual representation.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:37.087
Now, because it was Evans,

00:21:37.087 --> 00:21:39.390
when he said non-conceptual
representation,

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:42.690
he was obviously character
rising it as non-conceptual with

00:21:42.690 --> 00:21:45.360
respect to his own
definition of conceptuality.

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:48.570
So it was the algebraic, you know,

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:51.300
some kind of algebraic
combination of parts,

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:54.180
A is F, B is H, and so on and so forth.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:55.173
Not to McDowell's.

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:59.250
Is introduction of the notion
of non-conceptual content

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:03.870
led to quite a debate about
what non-conceptual is,

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:05.940
non-conceptual content is.

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:08.520
The fact that it's negatively
defined doesn't help.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:10.680
There's a question of whether
you could have non-conceptual

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:12.810
judgments in thought,
or only in perception.

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:14.130
You could have a question

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:16.410
of whether they just occurred in action.

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:18.450
There was a question of whether
creatures that don't have

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:19.500
conceptual powers,

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:22.530
like simple animals and
stuff can actually exhib--

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:25.487
Can actually have a
non-conceptual judgment

00:22:25.487 --> 00:22:26.637
and so on and so forth.

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:28.590
What are some examples though,

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:29.520
to give you a feel?

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:31.320
Evan's had this example
of being in this study

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:32.850
late at night.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:33.930
And they're being a s--

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:35.160
Thinking he's completely alone

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:37.110
and there's a step on the floor,

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:39.120
unmistakable step on the
floor right behind him,

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:40.653
and he freezes in terror.

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:45.660
Now, he knows exactly where
that footstep happened, right?

00:22:45.660 --> 00:22:48.123
It's just his imagination is riveted.

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:51.870
But, he doesn't have conceptual resources

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:53.790
with which to describe that space.

00:22:53.790 --> 00:22:57.870
It's not like 137.6 degrees to my right

00:22:57.870 --> 00:23:01.200
and 2.1 meters behind me
or something like that.

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:02.190
It just wasn't--

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:04.413
It wasn't given to him in conceptual form.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:08.353
And yet it was,

00:23:08.353 --> 00:23:10.410
it had content and it was very precise.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:11.940
Similarly, if I'm teaching tennis,

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:14.640
which God knows you don't
want to have happen,

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:15.570
but anyway,

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:19.200
suppose I tell someone to
hit their second serve,

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:21.030
suppose their second serve
is going out and I say,

00:23:21.030 --> 00:23:22.200
look, you're hitting it too hard.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.630
Just hit it at the speed
at which paper is ejected

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:26.370
from the big copier at the office,

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:28.380
I mean your department or something.

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:29.250
It's not clear.

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:30.270
I mean,

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:32.513
that's a speed and you know
that the paper is coming outta

00:23:32.513 --> 00:23:34.043
the copier at a certain speed,

00:23:34.043 --> 00:23:37.440
but, you don't know what
speed that is in any way that,

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:38.340
you know, like miles an hour.

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:39.173
And even if it did,

00:23:39.173 --> 00:23:40.950
it's not clear you would
be able to hit a serve

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:42.240
at that many miles an hour.

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:46.200
It's that form of understanding
of speed that you have both

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:50.400
for papers ejected from
copiers and for tennis serve.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:51.233
They're not,

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:54.690
they're not conceptually formulated.

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:58.290
Adrian Cousins a foster
who was a postdoc of mine

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:00.960
way back when in the eighties, I guess.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:03.900
He got a PhD in Oxford and he was stopped.

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:06.030
He rode a motorcycle late at night.

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:09.300
So, maybe the late at night
is a theme of this talk.

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:13.560
He was stopped by the police
and they hauled him up

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:14.910
and said, "Look,

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:16.710
do you know how fast you were going?

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:19.590
And Adrian said, "Well, in one way I know,

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:21.040
and in one way I don't know",

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:23.730
which I don't think was a
good thing to say to police.

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:25.950
But what he meant was he
knew how fast he was going

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:26.783
around the corner.

00:24:26.783 --> 00:24:29.430
It was wet and it was sandy,

00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:31.590
and he knew that he
could go only that fast

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:32.970
before skidding out.

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:35.070
He could probably do it again
and without a very small

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:38.580
margin of error, repeat the same velocity.

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:40.440
But it was not given to him conceptually,

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.927
he didn't know how many
kilometers per hour he was going

00:24:42.927 --> 00:24:44.370
and whether he was over the speed limit.

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:46.148
And probably he knew he
was over the speed limit.

00:24:46.148 --> 00:24:48.798
But, the point is his
sense of that speed was not

00:24:48.798 --> 00:24:52.710
conceptually articulated
and so on and so forth.

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:57.600
Evans argued that a great
deal of our knowledge,

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:00.180
especially perceptual knowledge,

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:03.603
is in this non-conceptual form.

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:09.360
Now, that notion of
non-conceptual content of Evans

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:12.483
did drive a bit of a wedge
through Evans and McDowell.

00:25:14.616 --> 00:25:16.016
A wedge not well understood.

00:25:17.460 --> 00:25:18.293
For example,

00:25:18.293 --> 00:25:19.380
so as you go to a furniture store,

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:20.950
and you are with your partner

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:23.437
and you say to them,

00:25:23.437 --> 00:25:25.530
"Look, hey, shall we buy those curtains?"

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:27.210
And your partner says, "No, look.

00:25:27.210 --> 00:25:29.217
That red won't go with our sofa."

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:32.910
Now, what does that red refer to?

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:34.623
It refers to a shade of red.

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:38.540
There are different shades of red.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:40.980
You know, some might be,

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:43.170
might come out blood in occult ceremonies

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:44.100
and burgundy wine.

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:45.600
Another might come out lipstick and

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:49.140
orlon sweaters, and muffy
at a prep school dance.

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:51.480
For Evans, those are different judgments.

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:54.120
And the differences matter
as regard conceptuality

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:55.203
cause they differ.

00:25:56.760 --> 00:25:59.100
Those differences matter
with respect to conceptuality

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:02.580
because how it is that those differences

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:06.603
manifest in your mind is not
conceptually explicable to you.

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:09.540
So, that judgment, that red,

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:11.340
is whatever, not the right one,

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:14.163
is not a conceptual
judgment on Evan's part.

00:26:15.180 --> 00:26:17.070
For McDowell, it's a
different judgment too.

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:19.170
But with respect to
defensive conceptuality,

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:21.930
he doesn't care because
that's a judgment about

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:23.190
the nature of the world.

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:25.020
Which is given to you in your
understanding of the world.

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:27.840
So, he thinks that's a
fine conceptual judgment.

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:30.693
So, crack started to appear
in this whole debate.

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:35.300
Which I'm not gonna go into.

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:39.090
I wanna push though, on the
notion of non-conceptual content

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:40.470
because if we go deeper,

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:43.320
I think we start to open
stuff up in ways that touch

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:46.833
contemporary AI and possibilities
for inference and stuff.

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:51.450
Okay, so part four, the
non-conceptual world.

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:55.103
I, myself have never
believed that the world

00:26:55.103 --> 00:26:56.853
of full was conceptual.

00:26:57.690 --> 00:27:01.030
It may have stemmed from my misspent youth

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:03.323
being...

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:08.253
Taking place in the Canadian north.

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:13.343
So...

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:19.140
So, I used to go paddling
around in Whitewater rivers.

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:21.030
And I just don't think
there are objects out there.

00:27:21.030 --> 00:27:22.969
I mean, of course there's
some objects, there's rocks.

00:27:22.969 --> 00:27:25.800
But, if we're talking about
re-identifiable particulars

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:26.880
under a category, you know,

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:30.360
what category rock exactly
is this and how many rocks

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:31.193
are there there?

00:27:31.193 --> 00:27:32.790
The thing underneath
the water on the right,

00:27:32.790 --> 00:27:34.410
is that one or is it 17?

00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:35.403
Or is that bedrock?

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:38.130
If you're gonna characterize rocks,

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:40.800
there's no sort of natural
boundary between the rocks

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:42.300
and pebbles and sand,

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:43.450
and god knows what all.

00:27:44.730 --> 00:27:46.740
One of the things that
you have to do in paddling

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:49.470
whitewater is you've got to go into the,

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:52.983
into the arrow of a V of dark
water pointing away from you.

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:57.060
And your life is often at
stake if you do this wrong.

00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:59.430
But, how many Vs are
there in this picture?

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:00.263
I don't know.

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:09.600
If you're paddling through
this maelstrom of spray

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:12.500
and waves and rocks and clouds
and all this kind of stuff,

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:16.119
there's no way to individuate.

00:28:16.119 --> 00:28:17.400
I mean, you may have enormous skill,

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:19.874
but there's no way to
individuate what's going on

00:28:19.874 --> 00:28:23.223
in ways that conceptual
representations require.

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:32.670
I think the way conceptual
representations work is different

00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:36.120
from how they are classically conceived.

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:39.510
And Priya, this actually gets
a little bit at your idea

00:28:39.510 --> 00:28:42.570
about whether this whole view
of conceptual representation

00:28:42.570 --> 00:28:44.703
is reductive.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.840
My sense is that conceptual
content informs a form of

00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:53.310
abstraction over a
fundamentally profuse richness

00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:55.260
in the underlying world.

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:59.490
That is essentially a
conceptual achievement

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:04.490
to box and construct categories
and so on and so forth.

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:09.000
In order to bring things
out there under a regiment

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:10.263
of intelligibility.

00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:17.120
And that's sort of the norm
on conceptual representation

00:29:17.570 --> 00:29:20.553
is to render the world
intelligible by abstraction.

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:23.730
Now, how does this work?

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.080
If you'll forgive me,
I'm gonna actually put up

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:28.103
another slide.

00:29:28.103 --> 00:29:29.124
Now, in this figure.

00:29:29.124 --> 00:29:30.750
This is a waterfall plot
for people who know about

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:31.583
such things.

00:29:31.583 --> 00:29:32.416
It doesn't matter.

00:29:32.416 --> 00:29:33.990
Suppose this...

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:36.731
Suppose this was a sort
of topographic set of

00:29:36.731 --> 00:29:39.240
vertical slices through a landscape

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:41.273
at the edge of a lake
or something like this.

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:44.700
And then suppose somebody says,

00:29:45.771 --> 00:29:47.130
"Look, we should go up
one of the two ridges

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:50.487
towards the left end of this picture."

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:54.360
What are they referring
to by the two ridges?

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:56.490
You know, one possibility is
that those are the two ridges

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:57.813
they're referring to.

00:29:58.872 --> 00:30:00.390
Another possibility is
that they're the left two

00:30:00.390 --> 00:30:01.860
are the ones that they're referring to,

00:30:01.860 --> 00:30:03.600
and they think the one on the right

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:07.170
is different from what it was
that the person on the right

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:09.153
thought and so on and so forth.

00:30:11.340 --> 00:30:13.890
So in other words, the category ridge,

00:30:13.890 --> 00:30:17.400
the property of being a ridge
here is being imposed by an

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:20.460
epistemic mind on an underlying world,

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:23.940
which is in fact much richer
than in fact those categories

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:25.533
can in fact do justice to.

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:28.383
Another example,

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:32.010
there's an artist by
the name of Adam Lowe,

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:34.350
a good friend of mine who--

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:36.533
And we've talked about
what the world's like.

00:30:38.125 --> 00:30:39.930
And he painted this picture.

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:41.520
This is meant to be a realistic picture,

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:45.690
but in the following sense
that if our perception

00:30:45.690 --> 00:30:47.610
renders the world's richness

00:30:47.610 --> 00:30:51.150
intelligible by our packaging it up and

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:54.030
labeling it and categorizing
it and smoothing the boundaries

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:55.180
and so on and so forth.

00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:58.500
He couldn't paint a picture
of what the world was like

00:30:58.500 --> 00:31:01.440
and to make any point because
our perceptual apparatus

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:02.550
would be looking at the picture.

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:04.860
And it would just construct the picture

00:31:04.860 --> 00:31:07.287
in categories and smooth
out the boundaries

00:31:07.287 --> 00:31:08.120
and so on and so forth.

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:09.630
Then we'd just love to look at picture.

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:11.490
So, he's not an algebraist,

00:31:11.490 --> 00:31:13.440
but to put this in algebraic terms,

00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:16.920
if you assume that perception
computes F of a scene,

00:31:16.920 --> 00:31:19.080
what he's painted here is a conception,

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:21.690
his conception of F to the
minus one of that scene.

00:31:21.690 --> 00:31:22.523
In other words,

00:31:22.523 --> 00:31:27.523
this differs from reality by
the amount by which we in fact

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:29.550
smooth out reality in order to fit it into

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:32.850
our conceptual scheme so that
when our perception sees this,

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:35.340
it's actually given to us

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:38.130
in our judgment as something
like the raw material

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:40.630
with respect to which we
see the world in general.

00:31:42.134 --> 00:31:45.210
And what's interesting about
this is I can completely read

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:46.920
this picture, I don't know if you can.

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:47.940
And I apologize,

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:50.070
it's probably not the best on this,

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:50.903
on Zoom.

00:31:50.903 --> 00:31:52.020
But, I'll just point out something.

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:55.420
There's a door over here
with some lumber inside

00:31:56.684 --> 00:31:59.580
and a step ladder leaning over and stuff.

00:31:59.580 --> 00:32:00.510
You can sort of tell that.

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:01.860
Down here there's a pail.

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:04.320
That's not too hard to see.

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:08.640
On the right hand, what's it called?

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:10.770
Anyway, the right hand
side of the doorway.

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:14.830
There's a mobile phone, a
1980s kind of mobile phone

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:19.149
anchored to the right hand post there.

00:32:19.149 --> 00:32:20.103
(coughs) Excuse me.

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:23.513
There's a box,

00:32:23.513 --> 00:32:25.260
yeah, in there inside the room

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:28.357
with lots of sticks of kindling sized wood

00:32:28.357 --> 00:32:29.190
and so on and so forth.

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:32.937
And over here is a
person crossing over to--

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:36.090
And actually to pick up the phone

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:38.118
it turns out from other
aspects of this picture

00:32:38.118 --> 00:32:39.173
and so on and so forth.

00:32:41.370 --> 00:32:44.010
You don't necessarily read
these things as door and pale

00:32:44.010 --> 00:32:46.107
and box and person and so on and so forth.

00:32:46.107 --> 00:32:48.120
And telephone immediately.

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:51.700
But, what he is sort of
claiming this picture is

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:55.560
we actually do, do that
kind of classification

00:32:55.560 --> 00:32:57.120
and categorization on inputs.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:32:58.830
And one of the things
that's troubling about AI

00:32:58.830 --> 00:33:03.570
is when AI first put television
cameras on systems back,

00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:06.060
you know, 150 years ago when I was young.

00:33:06.060 --> 00:33:09.120
People were astounded by
the messiness of the world

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:13.080
that the cameras that
were the camera outputs

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:15.270
because they thought that in
fact the camera was looking at

00:33:15.270 --> 00:33:18.000
like nice scenes of, you know,

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:22.440
nice scenes of vases of flowers on tables

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:24.346
or something like this.

00:33:24.346 --> 00:33:26.220
Still life.

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:27.053
And it was a mess.

00:33:27.053 --> 00:33:27.886
It was a mess.

00:33:27.886 --> 00:33:29.466
And people were astounded that, oh my god,

00:33:29.466 --> 00:33:31.953
we deal with this mess.

00:33:35.457 --> 00:33:36.540
So...

00:33:40.800 --> 00:33:41.900
So, the point is this,

00:33:43.050 --> 00:33:45.063
this orientation says that, look,

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:49.080
the fact that in what
philosophy actually calls

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:50.010
our natural attitude,

00:33:50.010 --> 00:33:52.560
the fact that we take the
world to be conceptual.

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:54.390
To be consisting of objects and properties

00:33:54.390 --> 00:33:55.640
and categories and stuff.

00:33:56.970 --> 00:34:00.360
That's not how it actually
is in and of itself.

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:02.220
It's a human achievement.

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:05.640
The fact that it looks
like it is that way.

00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:08.190
And in fact a lot of philosophy
has been distracted into

00:34:08.190 --> 00:34:10.560
thinking that it just is that way,

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:13.920
aboriginal, is because that, in fact,

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:15.900
has been processed by the very cognition

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:18.210
that we're trying to understand.

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:20.310
So, okay, so suppose I tell
this to students and they say,

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:21.710
well, what else could it be?

00:34:22.890 --> 00:34:24.750
Now, it's hard to answer that in language,

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:27.630
especially in intellectual
context like AI courses

00:34:27.630 --> 00:34:30.300
and philosophy courses
and so on and so forth.

00:34:30.300 --> 00:34:32.732
In intellectual context
such as science philosophy

00:34:32.732 --> 00:34:33.565
and so on and so forth,

00:34:33.565 --> 00:34:34.740
in which people assume
that language is conceptual

00:34:34.740 --> 00:34:37.053
in the sense that we talked about before.

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:41.310
It would not be as hard
to convey in a sense,

00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:43.560
in a classroom or a community or something

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:46.200
of people who were not themselves.

00:34:46.200 --> 00:34:48.090
Scientists or philosophers
or AI people and stuff.

00:34:48.090 --> 00:34:50.910
If it was a, you know,

00:34:50.910 --> 00:34:52.590
class of poets or something like that,

00:34:52.590 --> 00:34:53.790
it would be easier, I think,

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:56.280
to get them to realize that in fact,

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:58.770
the world doesn't come chopped
up in this corpuscular way

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:01.113
that Nunberg talked about it.

00:35:04.500 --> 00:35:07.350
So, how can I convey to you
what the world might be like?

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:10.410
Well, I wanna make one suggestion,

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:11.550
I'm gonna make other suggestions

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:12.660
before the end of this talk.

00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:14.760
And this first suggestion
is just by way of

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:18.660
prying open this possibility.

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:20.860
I'm not trying to put
too much weight on it.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:24.960
But in a 1964 book called "Individuals",

00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:27.210
Peter Braunstein introduced
the notion of a feature,

00:35:27.210 --> 00:35:28.440
I mean not exactly introduced,

00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:30.210
but I mean developed
the notion of a feature,

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:32.430
as something more basic
and less committing,

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:34.560
less ontologically committing,

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:35.590
than a property

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:42.410
as something that can be
exemplified in space and time.

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:44.640
Manifested in space and time

00:35:44.640 --> 00:35:47.590
without actually needing an
object for its exemplification.

00:35:48.438 --> 00:35:49.683
(Coughs) Excuse me.

00:35:51.120 --> 00:35:54.183
So, suppose I say it's
raining or we say, it's foggy.

00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:59.610
Everybody knows that there's
no 'it' that is foggy.

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:01.770
Or no, 'it', that's raining.

00:36:01.770 --> 00:36:02.940
It's a kind of linguistic.

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:05.430
It's actually called "the
weather it" in linguistics.

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:07.030
It's an 'it' that doesn't refer.

00:36:10.110 --> 00:36:13.770
But the idea of features is that,

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:15.960
look, the features are just manifested

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:17.740
in absolutely rich ways

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:19.923
throughout the world.

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:23.910
Cross-cutting and in vortices
and so on and so forth,

00:36:23.910 --> 00:36:26.490
without actually requiring
object identities

00:36:26.490 --> 00:36:27.960
underneath them.

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:30.450
One way to imagine this is to
imagine falling war boarding

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:34.410
in a storm at sea surrounded
by nothing but crashing waves

00:36:34.410 --> 00:36:36.420
and stinging spray and undulating currents

00:36:36.420 --> 00:36:38.490
and so on and so forth as
far as the eye can see.

00:36:38.490 --> 00:36:39.480
And then subtract you,

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:41.330
so there are no objects there at all.

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:45.794
I think that in fact
the world of physics--

00:36:45.794 --> 00:36:46.985
Now, Priya's a physicist.

00:36:46.985 --> 00:36:51.600
So, I look forward to her comment on this.

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:54.150
I actually think that's kind
of what the world is like

00:36:54.150 --> 00:36:55.323
according to physics.

00:36:57.360 --> 00:37:00.600
You might not think that it's that way

00:37:00.600 --> 00:37:04.110
because physics, in fact,

00:37:04.110 --> 00:37:07.020
often talks in terms of objects.

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:09.060
You know, there's a mass of
three kilograms traveling at

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:11.760
this, four meters per second
off slides of a table,

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:13.110
a meter high, where does it land?

00:37:13.110 --> 00:37:14.677
And so on and so forth.

00:37:15.570 --> 00:37:16.890
The various objects in this situation,

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:18.865
a mass of three kilograms,
a table and so on.

00:37:18.865 --> 00:37:23.280
But those objects are not
ontological commitments

00:37:23.280 --> 00:37:24.240
of physics.

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:28.080
Those objects are epistemic
practices of doing physics,

00:37:28.080 --> 00:37:29.880
the theoretical enterprise,

00:37:29.880 --> 00:37:32.580
so that we can in fact
work out our equations.

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:36.390
But the equations don't
quantify over the objects.

00:37:36.390 --> 00:37:41.280
They quantify over the
measures of the properties

00:37:41.280 --> 00:37:43.653
or features that in fact are exemplified.

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:48.720
And even if you think about
the gravitational law,

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:52.260
G times M1 times M2, divided by R squared,

00:37:52.260 --> 00:37:53.763
I think if I recall correctly.

00:37:55.620 --> 00:37:58.170
One of the things you do in
your first year physics class,

00:37:58.170 --> 00:38:01.350
at least I did 150 years ago,

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:04.290
was you prove that in
fact you can treat masses

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:07.920
as point masses at the location
of the center of gravity

00:38:07.920 --> 00:38:11.340
for purposes of calculating
gravitational attraction.

00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:13.050
But, that's not ontological commitment

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:14.130
of the physics underlying it.

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:16.440
The ontological commitments of physics

00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:19.290
underlying it is that in
fact masses, the, you know,

00:38:19.290 --> 00:38:22.800
it's a density field
spread out over a region.

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:25.440
And if you really wanted to
express the law of gravity

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:28.260
in a way that actually didn't
make this epistemic assumption

00:38:28.260 --> 00:38:30.150
for the convenient physicists,

00:38:30.150 --> 00:38:31.710
you would have to express
the law of gravity

00:38:31.710 --> 00:38:33.960
as a double, triple integral
and so on and so forth.

00:38:33.960 --> 00:38:36.360
Because there aren't objects in physics.

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:37.560
I think that's right.

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:39.150
And I actually don't think
there are many objects

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:40.503
in science either.

00:38:41.520 --> 00:38:45.090
So, that will generate a lot of cortisol,

00:38:45.090 --> 00:38:48.003
I suspect, in many audience members.

00:38:49.380 --> 00:38:50.820
But I think that idea,

00:38:50.820 --> 00:38:52.890
I mean it goes with field
theoretical interpretations

00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:54.030
of physics more too.

00:38:54.030 --> 00:38:56.880
That idea that maybe you could
make sense of physics as a

00:38:56.880 --> 00:39:00.240
kind of characterization of features

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:03.120
placed continuously in the world.

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:05.280
The fact that that might be intelligible

00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:07.740
suggests that features
might be an intelligible way

00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:08.790
of taking the world to be,

00:39:08.790 --> 00:39:10.980
which means that in fact
conceptual ontology isn't

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:13.500
necessarily the only way to
take the ontological structure

00:39:13.500 --> 00:39:14.333
of the world.

00:39:14.333 --> 00:39:16.620
And that's really why I want them here.

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:18.603
However, features are problematic too.

00:39:19.980 --> 00:39:22.440
Because in fact, the whole
assumption of features,

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:26.160
like in field thetic interpretations
is that the feature is

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:28.080
the same feature that's
exemplified in lots of different

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:29.580
places and so on and so forth.

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:34.520
So, it's just a step
towards relinquishing the...

00:39:37.110 --> 00:39:41.250
The grip of the conceptual
ontology as a presupposition

00:39:41.250 --> 00:39:42.850
for how the world is structured.

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:47.913
I think a better way that we can go,

00:39:47.913 --> 00:39:50.490
or a way that we can
go deeper a little bit

00:39:50.490 --> 00:39:53.220
is to see how conceptual and
non-conceptual representation.

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:56.460
Okay, the two epistemic
practices we talked about

00:39:56.460 --> 00:39:59.220
might fit into a amount of
physical or ontological picture

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:02.470
of this sort of a world
of surpassing stupifying

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:07.473
richness and operate as
kinds of abstraction over it.

00:40:09.150 --> 00:40:11.193
So, four points.

00:40:13.260 --> 00:40:15.360
First point is that this is not,

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:17.760
and this is another form of reductionism,

00:40:17.760 --> 00:40:20.210
that this is not to go to
Priya's great question.

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:24.510
It is not a view that the
world is built up from atoms

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:26.700
or elementary pieces or
ultimate constituents

00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:27.780
of our ontological life.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:30.540
And that in fact, complex
or high level or something,

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:33.483
objects are in fact
assemblages of lower levels.

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:36.960
It's more like a Jamesian blooming,

00:40:36.960 --> 00:40:38.670
buzzing confusion conception of the world,

00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:41.550
but not in the sense which
James is often understood.

00:40:41.550 --> 00:40:43.380
It's that the blooming buzzing confusion

00:40:43.380 --> 00:40:44.550
is under if--

00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:46.023
Undifferentiated K-pop.

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:49.830
Rather, what I'm trying
to get at is an idea that

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:51.930
the world is sort of
infinitely heterogeneous,

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:53.760
stupefyingly complex.

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:56.070
And that we navigate and wrestle it into

00:40:56.070 --> 00:41:00.270
comprehensible form in order
to make our way in our,

00:41:00.270 --> 00:41:03.633
you know, in our poultry
resource limited lives.

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:07.713
So, another point,

00:41:08.850 --> 00:41:12.180
one merit of this view
that conceptual ontology

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:14.880
is an epistemic achievement
on top of a world

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:16.470
of surpassing richness,

00:41:16.470 --> 00:41:18.090
is that it provides a way to account for

00:41:18.090 --> 00:41:19.830
the most important insight, I think,

00:41:19.830 --> 00:41:22.590
and commitment underlying science studies,

00:41:22.590 --> 00:41:25.083
underlying feminist epistemology
and so on and so forth.

00:41:25.083 --> 00:41:26.880
That concepts are normative.

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:28.050
And not just concepts, but objects.

00:41:28.050 --> 00:41:29.940
They are the locus of value,

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:32.640
they're culturally relative,
all these kinds of stuff.

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:34.770
How does it make room for that?

00:41:34.770 --> 00:41:36.270
Because in fact,

00:41:36.270 --> 00:41:39.600
what it suggests is that
a conceptual abstraction

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:41.070
of the world,

00:41:41.070 --> 00:41:42.750
so a conceptual judgment is true

00:41:42.750 --> 00:41:45.510
when the thereby abstracted situation,

00:41:45.510 --> 00:41:48.270
what you take the ridge to
be and so on and so forth.

00:41:48.270 --> 00:41:49.740
Satisfies the norms,

00:41:49.740 --> 00:41:51.420
the dynamic norms governing the lives

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:53.020
of the creatures who perform it.

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:58.890
So, if Priya is a geologist,

00:41:58.890 --> 00:42:00.150
and I'm a mountain climber,

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:02.980
and I climb a peak

00:42:04.766 --> 00:42:07.270
in the Kluane wilderness
in Yukon where there's

00:42:08.231 --> 00:42:10.593
a Mount Ross that I think is 19,000 feet.

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:14.140
And I say, oh look, I've
climbed the highest peak in,

00:42:15.570 --> 00:42:16.440
you know, or whatever,

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:19.170
just the last peak over
8,000 meters or something.

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:20.510
It probably isn't 7,000
meters, something like that.

00:42:20.510 --> 00:42:21.343
It doesn't matter.

00:42:21.343 --> 00:42:22.507
I'm a philosopher,

00:42:22.507 --> 00:42:24.173
so I don't need the deal in pesky facts.

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:31.500
If I say I climbed all
of the 7,000 meter peaks,

00:42:31.500 --> 00:42:33.870
and Priya says, no, look,
you haven't because you're on

00:42:33.870 --> 00:42:37.200
this peak, but over there about
seven meters to the right,

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:39.450
it dips a little bit and then
there's another little rise.

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:40.980
And so that technically is a peak

00:42:40.980 --> 00:42:43.050
and you haven't been there.

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:46.110
Now I could say, look, that's
not what I mean by peak.

00:42:46.110 --> 00:42:51.110
That's not the category peak
that I'm using for my climbing.

00:42:51.810 --> 00:42:53.940
And it might be a category
peak that she's using

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:56.760
for her geological survey
or something like this.

00:42:56.760 --> 00:43:00.930
But the point is that, how to
understand my category of peak

00:43:00.930 --> 00:43:02.910
is with respect to mountain climbing,

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:04.530
and the norms that
govern mountain climbing

00:43:04.530 --> 00:43:05.813
and so on and so forth.

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:09.420
This is what the feminist
epistemologist I think are are on

00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:11.222
about incorrectly so that--

00:43:11.222 --> 00:43:12.136
- Brian.
- Yeah.

00:43:12.136 --> 00:43:12.993
- Quick question.
- Yes.

00:43:12.993 --> 00:43:16.020
- But, there needs to
be a consensus, right?

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:17.850
On the definition of peak,

00:43:17.850 --> 00:43:20.790
even amongst just the mountain climbers.

00:43:20.790 --> 00:43:21.623
- Well,

00:43:21.623 --> 00:43:24.300
- Or do you need the
definition of a peak to be

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:25.890
some part of objective reality

00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:28.440
that cannot be contested by anyone?

00:43:28.440 --> 00:43:29.273
- Well, I--

00:43:31.350 --> 00:43:32.853
Low there be lawyers.

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:38.160
I actually don't think that there are--

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:41.490
I mean there the structure of agreement

00:43:41.490 --> 00:43:44.970
and consensuality and communion
and so on and so forth,

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:46.920
that's a huge topic in science studies

00:43:46.920 --> 00:43:49.260
as to how that stuff gets worked out.

00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:52.203
There was a famous case of
a racehorse which crossed,

00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:55.710
and as to which horse won the race.

00:43:55.710 --> 00:43:58.380
And there was one horse
and its nose looked like

00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:00.870
it was further ahead than the one behind,

00:44:00.870 --> 00:44:03.600
but the one behind was like
an inch behind or something.

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:06.450
The rider argued that it had
won the race because in fact

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:09.240
there was spittle coming
out of the nostril

00:44:09.240 --> 00:44:10.590
of the horse that was behind.

00:44:10.590 --> 00:44:12.540
And the spittle was
continuous with respect

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:15.120
to that horse and that the spittle

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:17.460
crossed the finish line before

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:18.930
the nose of the other one did.

00:44:18.930 --> 00:44:21.360
And so that constituted
that horse winning.

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:22.440
And it went to court.

00:44:22.440 --> 00:44:24.810
I mean, it went to court as
you could imagine such things.

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:26.730
I think the second horse won actually.

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:29.067
But, it doesn't matter
who won, it's that--

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:36.693
There has to be good enough
agreement for practices to work.

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:41.920
And I think it's just not true that

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:46.743
categories in general
get resolved, absolutely.

00:44:47.910 --> 00:44:48.900
There's politics.

00:44:48.900 --> 00:44:49.970
There's politics.

00:44:49.970 --> 00:44:51.990
And you know,

00:44:51.990 --> 00:44:54.990
sometimes there's been
really pernicious politics

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:56.190
behind these judgements.

00:44:56.190 --> 00:44:58.020
You know, who's a person and stuff,

00:44:58.020 --> 00:44:59.490
who gets to vote.

00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:01.740
You know, right now, with respect to

00:45:01.740 --> 00:45:03.030
whether in fact it's illegal to

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:05.700
put a pregnant woman in prison
because you're putting two

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:07.140
people in prison, you know, that's--

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:11.250
That category's absolutely
not well agreed and so on.

00:45:11.250 --> 00:45:14.425
So, there'd be politics I think.

00:45:14.425 --> 00:45:15.432
- Okay.

00:45:15.432 --> 00:45:18.020
- But the point is this is a huge...

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:21.930
This is a huge issue in,

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:23.157
I mean this is something we could talk to

00:45:23.157 --> 00:45:24.513
Joe Ress about tomorrow.

00:45:26.460 --> 00:45:29.370
The sort of politics of the negotiation

00:45:29.370 --> 00:45:31.200
of conceptual agreement
and so on and so forth,

00:45:31.200 --> 00:45:32.590
in different fields.

00:45:32.590 --> 00:45:33.640
A big topic, I think.

00:45:35.940 --> 00:45:36.773
- Thank you.

00:45:36.773 --> 00:45:38.663
- Here really is just that the...

00:45:41.301 --> 00:45:42.510
The metaphysical picture here,

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:45.120
that in fact the concepts
and categories and stuff

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:48.903
are abstractions in terms of
which we try to fit reality.

00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:50.930
Is that...

00:45:54.480 --> 00:45:55.313
Okay.

00:45:57.180 --> 00:45:59.433
Are they out there in the objective world?

00:46:00.540 --> 00:46:01.373
Well...

00:46:06.960 --> 00:46:07.833
Here's the thing.

00:46:10.020 --> 00:46:13.110
It's generally accepted in philosophy that

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:14.610
semantics needs naturalizing.

00:46:14.610 --> 00:46:17.010
We need an account of semantics
of how reference works

00:46:17.010 --> 00:46:20.013
and how thought works
and so on and so forth.

00:46:20.013 --> 00:46:22.200
What I'm saying here is that ontology,

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:24.570
in the sense of conceptual ontology,

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:28.440
what an object is that
needs naturalizing too.

00:46:28.440 --> 00:46:31.112
Because the process of abstraction

00:46:31.112 --> 00:46:32.730
that underwrite the
grouping and individuation

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:34.800
into individuals and so on and so forth,

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:37.260
which is essential to our
understanding of cognition,

00:46:37.260 --> 00:46:40.620
are no less mysterious and no
more secured by a mechanistic

00:46:40.620 --> 00:46:42.510
or causal scientific worldview.

00:46:42.510 --> 00:46:44.490
No more autonomously integrable

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:46.260
with results in your temporary science

00:46:46.260 --> 00:46:48.360
than any other of the
challenges and features

00:46:48.360 --> 00:46:49.350
of intentionality.

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:50.940
So for example, if an amoeba splits,

00:46:50.940 --> 00:46:53.520
is that two new amoeba, or
is one of them same one.

00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:56.760
Or is it one that is dually
instantiated in the world?

00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:59.820
If you have a redwood tree and
there's all kinds of fronds,

00:46:59.820 --> 00:47:01.500
is that what they're called
growing up out of the roots?

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:03.270
And some of these things
can be 30 feet tall

00:47:03.270 --> 00:47:04.560
and so on and so forth.

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:05.870
Are those lots of different redwood trees

00:47:05.870 --> 00:47:07.830
or the same redwood tree?

00:47:07.830 --> 00:47:10.350
You know, science remarkably
doesn't care about

00:47:10.350 --> 00:47:12.090
such things, I think.

00:47:12.090 --> 00:47:13.043
Because I don't think, as I said before,

00:47:13.043 --> 00:47:14.820
I don't think science
is actually committed

00:47:14.820 --> 00:47:16.533
to conceptual ontology.

00:47:17.691 --> 00:47:21.180
And I think to assume as so
much of philosophy and logic

00:47:21.180 --> 00:47:22.680
and XML and so on and so forth,

00:47:22.680 --> 00:47:25.650
assume that the world of objects,

00:47:25.650 --> 00:47:28.050
properties and so on
exists independently of us

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:30.570
and objectively and so on and so forth.

00:47:30.570 --> 00:47:32.400
is essentially to suggest that the world

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:34.323
is autonomously conceptual.

00:47:35.460 --> 00:47:38.910
And that's an especially
pernicious way of succumbing

00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:41.333
what's called the myth of
the given, in philosophy.

00:47:42.780 --> 00:47:43.613
But...

00:47:46.980 --> 00:47:48.930
But, and this is an important but,

00:47:48.930 --> 00:47:51.003
and it's really, really tough to convey.

00:47:54.630 --> 00:47:57.900
On this picture, conceptual content, okay?

00:47:57.900 --> 00:48:00.090
The epistemic nature of conceptuality.

00:48:00.090 --> 00:48:02.820
So, the nature of that I
talked about in the conceptual

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:04.290
representation part of the talk,

00:48:04.290 --> 00:48:06.450
not the conceptual
world, part of the talk.

00:48:06.450 --> 00:48:08.670
And it's the conceptual
representation part that's relevant

00:48:08.670 --> 00:48:11.700
to logic involves loss.

00:48:11.700 --> 00:48:13.800
That's what abstraction means, throw away.

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:17.250
You throw away vast amounts of detail.

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:18.083
Why?

00:48:18.083 --> 00:48:19.500
In order to support generalization

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:21.420
and long distance inference.

00:48:21.420 --> 00:48:22.740
So, for example,

00:48:22.740 --> 00:48:24.770
one thing I learned when
I moved to California is,

00:48:24.770 --> 00:48:27.090
in New England, if you wander
around in the countryside

00:48:27.090 --> 00:48:28.680
and you get lost.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:29.670
If you wanna find a road,

00:48:29.670 --> 00:48:31.410
you go down to the bottom of the valleys

00:48:31.410 --> 00:48:33.570
cause that's where the roads are.

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:35.250
In California, when
you're wandering around

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:37.530
outside San Francisco in
the Santa Cruz mountains,

00:48:37.530 --> 00:48:38.430
and you wanna find a road,

00:48:38.430 --> 00:48:39.810
you go to the top of the ridge.

00:48:39.810 --> 00:48:41.880
They're not at the bottom of the ridge.

00:48:41.880 --> 00:48:44.850
Now that's a generalization and
that generalizes over all of

00:48:44.850 --> 00:48:47.850
the differences between these
ridges and so on and so forth.

00:48:49.560 --> 00:48:54.090
Generalizations of the sort
that fit into logical inference

00:48:54.090 --> 00:48:57.513
require letting go of
massive amounts of detail.

00:49:02.062 --> 00:49:05.730
But, what about the ridge

00:49:05.730 --> 00:49:07.830
that in fact, we
identified in that picture.

00:49:07.830 --> 00:49:09.690
Or the ridge that I climb up

00:49:09.690 --> 00:49:12.093
in order to find a road
or something like that.

00:49:14.730 --> 00:49:17.080
Think of the ridge is in
those waterfall plots.

00:49:18.210 --> 00:49:21.630
Even if I register as an object

00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:23.910
by abstracting away from the details,

00:49:23.910 --> 00:49:26.643
the ridge itself is
not thereby abstracted.

00:49:27.900 --> 00:49:29.910
And the reference of the phrase that ridge

00:49:29.910 --> 00:49:32.853
is not necessarily abstracted either.

00:49:33.780 --> 00:49:34.613
So if I say,

00:49:34.613 --> 00:49:35.460
look, I'm gonna climb that ridge

00:49:35.460 --> 00:49:37.650
and you saying, look,
you're never gonna make it.

00:49:37.650 --> 00:49:40.920
You may be referring to the
richness of the ridge itself,

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:44.313
not to the richness of
it registered as a ridge.

00:49:46.290 --> 00:49:47.550
Go back to that picture of whitewater

00:49:47.550 --> 00:49:48.930
I had at the beginning.

00:49:48.930 --> 00:49:50.580
If you argue that, I mean,

00:49:50.580 --> 00:49:54.360
another part of philosophy
talks about issues

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:55.758
of vagueness,

00:49:55.758 --> 00:49:58.500
that words like rapid or
eddie or something are vague,

00:49:58.500 --> 00:49:59.940
because maybe there's no facts,

00:49:59.940 --> 00:50:02.580
no absolute facts of the
matter as to how many rapids

00:50:02.580 --> 00:50:05.700
are at ease or something
there are in that picture.

00:50:05.700 --> 00:50:07.470
But again, the vagueness,

00:50:07.470 --> 00:50:09.030
I think it's mistake to call it vagueness.

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:11.403
But even if it were okay
to call it vagueness,

00:50:12.870 --> 00:50:14.730
there's nothing vague about the river.

00:50:14.730 --> 00:50:16.950
If you're paddling down that
river and your life's at stake,

00:50:16.950 --> 00:50:17.783
there's nothing--

00:50:17.783 --> 00:50:19.050
You don't run into anything vague.

00:50:19.050 --> 00:50:23.163
You run into extremely concrete detail.

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:26.643
What's vague, if anything is,

00:50:29.430 --> 00:50:32.730
is our epistemic description
of it as a eddie or a rapid

00:50:32.730 --> 00:50:35.104
or something like this.

00:50:35.104 --> 00:50:37.893
And more importantly,

00:50:40.560 --> 00:50:44.100
it's interesting that if
you want to actually act,

00:50:44.100 --> 00:50:46.230
if you wanna put your finger just there

00:50:46.230 --> 00:50:47.490
where the wind is strongest,

00:50:47.490 --> 00:50:49.980
or crack the pattern of
animal motion against

00:50:49.980 --> 00:50:51.393
a background of grasses,

00:50:52.433 --> 00:50:54.540
or flip through of crack
in a wall or something.

00:50:54.540 --> 00:50:59.313
Then the rich detail is
necessary for fine grain action.

00:51:01.252 --> 00:51:03.570
In other words, the
super fluidity of detail,

00:51:03.570 --> 00:51:04.920
because it's so detailed,

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:07.830
is not good for long distance
reasoning and planning

00:51:07.830 --> 00:51:10.530
or inference, but it's
absolutely necessary for action.

00:51:10.530 --> 00:51:13.710
So, suppose I wanna reach
for a cup here of coffee,

00:51:13.710 --> 00:51:15.750
which I don't have, but unfortunately.

00:51:15.750 --> 00:51:17.808
And I say, okay, I'm gonna pick
up this cup and I reach out.

00:51:17.808 --> 00:51:20.520
Now, if you look at slow motion videos

00:51:20.520 --> 00:51:22.590
of this kind of stuff,
it's kind of remarkable.

00:51:22.590 --> 00:51:24.360
I may think that I'm
gonna pick up the cup,

00:51:24.360 --> 00:51:25.950
if that's a conceptual thought.

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:27.600
And it may be that the conceptual thought

00:51:27.600 --> 00:51:29.610
leads to my hand in that direction.

00:51:29.610 --> 00:51:31.500
But as I get close to it,

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:33.240
there are sub-conceptual representations

00:51:33.240 --> 00:51:36.420
that kind of take over and
adjust my fingers and my hand

00:51:36.420 --> 00:51:39.300
and orient my hand and so on and so forth.

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:41.280
Through non-conceptual representation,

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:44.760
so as to be prepared to more
exactly know how they should

00:51:44.760 --> 00:51:46.983
pick the cup up so that I don't dump it.

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:50.130
And the same thing for serving tennis

00:51:50.130 --> 00:51:52.830
or meeting in the corner on
a motorcycle or something.

00:51:55.620 --> 00:51:57.993
So, to try to wrap up here a little bit,

00:52:02.280 --> 00:52:05.190
representing or otherwise
dealing with the local

00:52:05.190 --> 00:52:07.920
ever so rich feature array,

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:10.800
or even more stupifying
detail in the world

00:52:10.800 --> 00:52:12.540
is good for controlling action

00:52:12.540 --> 00:52:14.970
and good for responding to fine detail,

00:52:14.970 --> 00:52:17.673
but it's bad for long distance
imprints in generality.

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:23.220
This leads to a picture of what
I call metaphysical monism,

00:52:23.220 --> 00:52:25.053
but ontological pluralism.

00:52:25.890 --> 00:52:28.050
Because what happens is we people,

00:52:28.050 --> 00:52:30.570
maybe other creatures too,

00:52:30.570 --> 00:52:33.450
live in a continuous
creative tension between

00:52:33.450 --> 00:52:36.060
the incredible richness of the world.

00:52:36.060 --> 00:52:38.820
And the long distance utility
of language abstraction

00:52:38.820 --> 00:52:40.170
and inference on the other.

00:52:41.550 --> 00:52:42.750
When we engage with the world,

00:52:42.750 --> 00:52:44.400
we wanna do the operative abstraction,

00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:47.220
we want to kind of reconcile
or concretize our ideas

00:52:47.220 --> 00:52:49.590
with respect to reality
to let more of the world's

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:52.170
ineffable details fill our representations

00:52:52.170 --> 00:52:54.620
in order to be appropriately
responsive in action

00:52:56.003 --> 00:52:58.320
to the world's fine grain detail.

00:52:58.320 --> 00:53:00.330
But when we want to travel long distances,

00:53:00.330 --> 00:53:02.250
by which I mean maybe
conceptual long distances,

00:53:02.250 --> 00:53:04.110
we wanna figure out
where in fact we're gonna

00:53:04.110 --> 00:53:05.670
button mine in Northern Canada.

00:53:05.670 --> 00:53:08.340
We can go into where the neutrino
flux will be the highest,

00:53:08.340 --> 00:53:10.623
when the sunspot arrives or whatever.

00:53:11.652 --> 00:53:13.383
Then we have to, in fact,

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:15.503
to do science,

00:53:15.503 --> 00:53:17.790
we have to let go of a lot of detail

00:53:17.790 --> 00:53:20.550
and employees spare more efficient methods

00:53:20.550 --> 00:53:23.463
purposely designed for inferential travel.

00:53:24.630 --> 00:53:25.463
And if we're clever,

00:53:25.463 --> 00:53:28.050
and we're damn clever,
I think there's nothing

00:53:28.050 --> 00:53:31.083
more important about AI than
it teaches you humility.

00:53:32.160 --> 00:53:34.500
We must in fact do both.

00:53:34.500 --> 00:53:37.980
Basically, living in
this middle realm between

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:40.350
engaging with the detail,
letting go of the detail.

00:53:40.350 --> 00:53:42.390
Allowing different
forms of representation,

00:53:42.390 --> 00:53:45.061
conceptual or not, and so on and so forth,

00:53:45.061 --> 00:53:47.853
to allow us both to think and to act.

00:53:49.350 --> 00:53:51.610
And I sometimes think of

00:53:53.910 --> 00:53:55.080
objects, properties, and relations.

00:53:55.080 --> 00:53:58.464
In other words, the
conceptual material of ont--

00:53:58.464 --> 00:54:01.050
The material of conceptual ontology.

00:54:01.050 --> 00:54:02.520
I think of the objects,
properties and relations,

00:54:02.520 --> 00:54:03.360
the things we started with,

00:54:03.360 --> 00:54:06.480
as the long distance trucks
and interstate highway systems

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:08.433
of intentional normative life.

00:54:09.270 --> 00:54:11.520
undeniably essential
to overall integration

00:54:11.520 --> 00:54:12.753
of life's practices.

00:54:13.620 --> 00:54:15.870
Crucial, given finite
resources for us to integrate

00:54:15.870 --> 00:54:17.730
the vast open-ended terrain of experience

00:54:17.730 --> 00:54:20.460
into a single, cohesive, objective world.

00:54:20.460 --> 00:54:23.550
But the cost of packaging
them up for portability

00:54:23.550 --> 00:54:25.470
and long-distance travel,

00:54:25.470 --> 00:54:27.930
is that it insulates them
from the extraordinary

00:54:27.930 --> 00:54:31.470
fine great richness of the
particular indigenous life,

00:54:31.470 --> 00:54:34.830
the ineffable richness of
the very lives they sustain.

00:54:34.830 --> 00:54:38.220
And what's magic about us
is that we live this dance

00:54:38.220 --> 00:54:39.600
between them, among them,

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:41.913
so as to both live and be able to think.

00:54:43.380 --> 00:54:46.620
Okay, so that's the end of part four.

00:54:46.620 --> 00:54:49.350
And if I have two more minutes,

00:54:49.350 --> 00:54:51.780
I'm just gonna say this epilogue,

00:54:51.780 --> 00:54:53.550
which I think is kinda interesting.

00:54:53.550 --> 00:54:54.750
This--

00:54:54.750 --> 00:54:56.880
Think about any AI,
contemporary AI systems, right?

00:54:56.880 --> 00:54:58.290
They end up doing inference,

00:54:58.290 --> 00:55:00.260
which is not obviously conceptual.

00:55:00.260 --> 00:55:03.300
And you know, you end up with vectors,

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:05.730
you know, 10,000 dimensional
vectors or something

00:55:05.730 --> 00:55:07.080
with real numbers and so on and so forth.

00:55:07.080 --> 00:55:08.130
And it just,

00:55:08.130 --> 00:55:09.830
you have this incredible richness.

00:55:11.430 --> 00:55:14.340
There's a huge amount of pressure
to make explicable, right?

00:55:14.340 --> 00:55:15.870
To explain these things.

00:55:15.870 --> 00:55:16.950
Which basically means,

00:55:16.950 --> 00:55:20.163
look, render these judgments
into conceptual form.

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:23.100
And I think part of the
picture I'm arguing is,

00:55:23.100 --> 00:55:23.933
look, that's--

00:55:23.933 --> 00:55:25.980
You gotta be careful about that.

00:55:25.980 --> 00:55:28.218
I know Priya, when I see her,

00:55:28.218 --> 00:55:31.050
and I think if I ran into
her in the streets of Miami

00:55:31.050 --> 00:55:32.350
where I didn't expect her.

00:55:33.480 --> 00:55:35.220
I would say, "Oh my god, Priya.

00:55:35.220 --> 00:55:36.210
You're, you're in Miami.

00:55:36.210 --> 00:55:37.310
What brings you here?"

00:55:38.430 --> 00:55:39.937
And then if somebody comes
up behind me and says,

00:55:39.937 --> 00:55:40.950
"How do you know that's Priya?"

00:55:40.950 --> 00:55:42.363
I haven't a bloody clue.

00:55:43.530 --> 00:55:45.330
Because my recognition of Priya as Priya

00:55:45.330 --> 00:55:48.150
is in fact not conceptually
available to me.

00:55:48.150 --> 00:55:51.000
It's probably not actually
in conceptual form at all,

00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:52.380
because I probably don't have concepts

00:55:52.380 --> 00:55:54.446
that are actually adequate
to the detail that I use

00:55:54.446 --> 00:55:58.143
to in fact, recognize
this particular person.

00:56:01.140 --> 00:56:02.463
So, how do we explain?

00:56:03.690 --> 00:56:05.163
Well I've got one more slide.

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:10.710
Here's a very short
sentence, just 23 words.

00:56:10.710 --> 00:56:13.650
It's the first sentence of
Henry James' Portrait of a Lady,

00:56:13.650 --> 00:56:16.650
which for some reason I
remembered all my life.

00:56:16.650 --> 00:56:17.700
Under certain circumstances,

00:56:17.700 --> 00:56:19.800
there are few hours in life
more agreeable than the hour

00:56:19.800 --> 00:56:22.803
dedicated to the ceremony
known as afternoon tea.

00:56:24.510 --> 00:56:26.310
Now, think about afternoon tea

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:29.253
about that, concept if you like.

00:56:31.290 --> 00:56:32.943
What I think is interesting is,

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:36.420
you know, our current models of logic

00:56:36.420 --> 00:56:38.550
would take afternoon tea
as referring to the concept

00:56:38.550 --> 00:56:39.383
of afternoon tea,

00:56:39.383 --> 00:56:41.590
which is a concept of exemplified by all

00:56:42.690 --> 00:56:44.790
forms of afternoon tea.

00:56:44.790 --> 00:56:47.160
But that's not a God-given insight

00:56:47.160 --> 00:56:51.063
that that's necessarily
afternoon tea means or refers to.

00:56:52.140 --> 00:56:55.980
And you can think of James
as actually very clever.

00:56:55.980 --> 00:56:57.330
And you know, Steinbeck's another author

00:56:57.330 --> 00:56:58.163
who's amazing at this.

00:56:58.163 --> 00:57:03.030
Just with a few words, can
actually lead a concept

00:57:03.030 --> 00:57:05.010
to in fact, have a specificity

00:57:05.010 --> 00:57:07.953
that absolutely transcends
our ability to get at it

00:57:07.953 --> 00:57:09.573
with a single word.

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:12.210
And from AI point of view,

00:57:12.210 --> 00:57:14.038
you can see how this might work.

00:57:14.038 --> 00:57:18.150
If you get all of the other
variables arranged in such a way

00:57:18.150 --> 00:57:22.440
that they can sort of focus
in on what it is that this

00:57:22.440 --> 00:57:25.050
concept is getting at in
this particular circumstance

00:57:25.050 --> 00:57:28.680
that you're in fact abstracting
in certain sorts of ways.

00:57:28.680 --> 00:57:30.990
I actually think it
might be an inspiration

00:57:30.990 --> 00:57:32.643
as to how to explain things.

00:57:33.540 --> 00:57:35.848
So, if you said to some medical system,

00:57:35.848 --> 00:57:37.650
look, what do you think we should do?

00:57:37.650 --> 00:57:39.350
Why are you prescribing this drug?

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:42.060
Anxiety drug or something.

00:57:42.060 --> 00:57:43.530
And imagine the system saying, well look,

00:57:43.530 --> 00:57:46.080
it's as if this guy was
told that they were about to

00:57:46.080 --> 00:57:47.850
experience a huge shock,

00:57:47.850 --> 00:57:50.100
as if waiting for a
diagnosis after surgery,

00:57:50.100 --> 00:57:52.230
but they actually have only
the vaguest premonition

00:57:52.230 --> 00:57:54.420
of what it's gonna be about.

00:57:54.420 --> 00:57:55.320
Or she's gonna be in a--

00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:57.630
She's in a state reminiscent
of the one that her sister

00:57:57.630 --> 00:58:00.270
experienced after sailing
single-handedly across the roaring

00:58:00.270 --> 00:58:02.553
forties on her solo
trip to Antarctic or so.

00:58:05.040 --> 00:58:08.460
Go back to what Nunberg said
about how the chopping up of

00:58:08.460 --> 00:58:10.470
narrative into corpuscular bits

00:58:10.470 --> 00:58:12.870
at the beginning of the
age for information,

00:58:12.870 --> 00:58:16.030
gave us this notion of
corpuscular recombinable

00:58:18.960 --> 00:58:20.350
corpuscular data

00:58:21.660 --> 00:58:24.340
that somehow we're trying to mine it for

00:58:25.320 --> 00:58:27.510
the richness of the world.

00:58:27.510 --> 00:58:29.040
That might be wrong.

00:58:29.040 --> 00:58:32.400
It might be that we need to realize that

00:58:32.400 --> 00:58:37.400
novels and poetry and
letters and that narrative

00:58:38.400 --> 00:58:41.850
is actually a locus of the understanding

00:58:41.850 --> 00:58:44.610
that fine tunes things in such a way

00:58:44.610 --> 00:58:47.790
that you can get a better sense
of the richness of the world

00:58:47.790 --> 00:58:49.800
that these things are about.

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:52.020
And what would be the
consequence of actually

00:58:52.020 --> 00:58:55.200
taking that on board as an
epistemic ontological assumption

00:58:55.200 --> 00:58:56.343
for the future of AI?

00:58:59.040 --> 00:58:59.873
I don't know.

00:59:01.530 --> 00:59:04.803
But, I appreciate your attention.

00:59:05.700 --> 00:59:06.873
Thanks very much.

00:59:08.760 --> 00:59:10.170
- Thank you so much, Brian

00:59:10.170 --> 00:59:12.813
for this absolutely wonderful talk,

00:59:13.830 --> 00:59:18.810
addressing so many
important deep issues in

00:59:18.810 --> 00:59:22.260
what constitutes conceptual understanding

00:59:22.260 --> 00:59:26.133
and therefore informs
inferences there from.

00:59:27.060 --> 00:59:28.392
That was a really well--
- Well, right.

00:59:28.392 --> 00:59:31.050
Sorry, could I interrupt
already? (chuckles)

00:59:31.050 --> 00:59:33.106
Priya knows that I'm not easily shut up.

00:59:33.106 --> 00:59:35.220
(Priya chuckles softly)

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:38.070
Our current models of
inference are conceptual.

00:59:38.070 --> 00:59:39.360
- Right. Right.

00:59:39.360 --> 00:59:42.660
- And I think that the
intriguing possibility

00:59:42.660 --> 00:59:45.240
is that second wave AI,

00:59:45.240 --> 00:59:47.370
although we don't understand how,

00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:48.870
is showing this inference

00:59:48.870 --> 00:59:51.630
may actually not need to be conceptual.

00:59:51.630 --> 00:59:54.300
And we may not know how to explain it,

00:59:54.300 --> 00:59:56.970
but it may show us that
inference can in fact

00:59:56.970 --> 00:59:58.860
occupy other places in this realm.

00:59:58.860 --> 00:59:59.693
So, I just think, I mean,

00:59:59.693 --> 01:00:00.870
I don't think you probably
disagree with that,

01:00:00.870 --> 01:00:02.414
I just think it's interesting.

01:00:02.414 --> 01:00:03.641
- No, I don't disagree with that.

01:00:03.641 --> 01:00:04.474
Right.

01:00:04.474 --> 01:00:06.240
So, no, I know with you that
I have to be extra careful

01:00:06.240 --> 01:00:07.617
with the language, right?

01:00:07.617 --> 01:00:09.750
So, you always catch
me on that every time.

01:00:09.750 --> 01:00:11.790
So I want to--

01:00:11.790 --> 01:00:15.450
Why don't I start off
with one sort of question,

01:00:15.450 --> 01:00:16.590
burning question that I have

01:00:16.590 --> 01:00:18.210
and then open it up to the floor?

01:00:18.210 --> 01:00:21.270
So, I invite everyone to
please start typing in

01:00:21.270 --> 01:00:23.850
your questions in the chat.

01:00:23.850 --> 01:00:26.702
So, I noted when you mentioned,

01:00:26.702 --> 01:00:28.800
you know, generalizations, right?

01:00:28.800 --> 01:00:33.800
So, generalizations require
discarding details, right?

01:00:34.530 --> 01:00:37.500
And hence information, right?

01:00:37.500 --> 01:00:41.687
And I wonder how this impacts

01:00:43.170 --> 01:00:46.720
what we can infer from

01:00:47.820 --> 01:00:50.580
generalized data, right?

01:00:50.580 --> 01:00:54.843
Do we lose or do we capture?

01:00:58.618 --> 01:01:02.670
So, do we manage to--

01:01:02.670 --> 01:01:04.660
Do we efficiently capture

01:01:05.820 --> 01:01:10.820
what is really needed
to robustly infer or?

01:01:11.460 --> 01:01:16.460
- Well, first of all,

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:19.320
I think there's no doubt
that generalizations

01:01:19.320 --> 01:01:22.140
are articulated with respect
to what I would call,

01:01:22.140 --> 01:01:23.190
registration schemes,

01:01:23.190 --> 01:01:24.900
which in fact ignore lots of details

01:01:24.900 --> 01:01:26.850
in order to get at
higher level regularities

01:01:26.850 --> 01:01:28.387
and so on and so forth.

01:01:33.543 --> 01:01:37.323
When we put those things in,

01:01:40.560 --> 01:01:42.993
in our system as it were,

01:01:44.340 --> 01:01:45.990
we may have other details.

01:01:45.990 --> 01:01:47.283
So if you think about,

01:01:48.930 --> 01:01:52.050
I mean really some quite
politically problematic

01:01:52.050 --> 01:01:54.123
systems that have determined,

01:01:55.050 --> 01:01:57.110
you know, recidivism rates
as to who should actually be

01:01:57.110 --> 01:01:59.220
allowed out of prison and who should not,

01:01:59.220 --> 01:02:01.677
or chances of success
in college, you know?

01:02:01.677 --> 01:02:03.810
And you know, all kinds of
other things about this person

01:02:03.810 --> 01:02:06.810
such as their median income

01:02:06.810 --> 01:02:08.277
and the nature of the
neighborhood they're in,

01:02:08.277 --> 01:02:10.653
and the racial profile
of the neighborhood.

01:02:17.250 --> 01:02:19.650
If we were doing inference
in a classical way,

01:02:19.650 --> 01:02:21.210
which is, say, a conceptual way,

01:02:21.210 --> 01:02:23.740
we might actually just

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:28.410
base that decision, let's say,

01:02:28.410 --> 01:02:31.350
about whether they should
be admitted to Yale

01:02:31.350 --> 01:02:33.960
on their grades or something like that.

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:35.970
But if you train an AI system,

01:02:35.970 --> 01:02:39.960
it's going to use all of the
other detail that it has.

01:02:39.960 --> 01:02:44.073
And that's probably not a
conceptually formulated filter.

01:02:45.330 --> 01:02:49.080
And the worry is that to some extent,

01:02:49.080 --> 01:02:50.040
well of course there are lots of worries.

01:02:50.040 --> 01:02:52.410
One is that, the result will be heinous

01:02:52.410 --> 01:02:56.195
in a whole variety of ways that
it'll reinscribe, you know,

01:02:56.195 --> 01:02:58.545
it'll reinscribe biases
that are already there.

01:02:59.670 --> 01:03:00.960
People struggle with fairness,

01:03:00.960 --> 01:03:03.218
they put in protected categories
and so on and so forth.

01:03:03.218 --> 01:03:04.418
But, you know, that's a,

01:03:05.460 --> 01:03:07.200
that's a very tough thing to do.

01:03:07.200 --> 01:03:08.280
I mean, it's not tough to--

01:03:08.280 --> 01:03:09.307
I mean, people do it,

01:03:09.307 --> 01:03:12.510
but ethically, it's kind
of complex to in fact,

01:03:12.510 --> 01:03:14.640
protect certain categories
because that's a form of

01:03:14.640 --> 01:03:15.783
prejudice also.

01:03:17.490 --> 01:03:20.070
And I think that the
challenge of these systems,

01:03:20.070 --> 01:03:21.840
I mean, one way to
understand the challenge

01:03:21.840 --> 01:03:24.190
of these systems, if you
read these books like,

01:03:25.867 --> 01:03:27.840
"Weapons of Mass Destruction",

01:03:27.840 --> 01:03:31.473
and, you know, a book I prefer actually.

01:03:33.150 --> 01:03:34.710
There the--

01:03:34.710 --> 01:03:37.020
By Brian Christian, what's it called?

01:03:37.020 --> 01:03:38.476
Anyway,--
- Yeah.

01:03:38.476 --> 01:03:39.667
I've read that.

01:03:39.667 --> 01:03:40.500
- "The Alignment Problem".

01:03:40.500 --> 01:03:41.610
Yeah, "The Alignment Problem".

01:03:41.610 --> 01:03:43.140
They document all kinds of problems.

01:03:43.140 --> 01:03:45.030
And sure enough, these are problems.

01:03:45.030 --> 01:03:47.553
But, what's going on with these problems?

01:03:50.370 --> 01:03:51.690
Well, I think partly--

01:03:51.690 --> 01:03:54.147
You know, and the fact that
you can't necessarily--

01:03:54.147 --> 01:03:55.710
You might think, look, I'm
not gonna put the person's

01:03:55.710 --> 01:03:59.430
racial background or something
is explicit variable,

01:03:59.430 --> 01:04:01.613
and then these systems
can figure it out anyway.

01:04:06.686 --> 01:04:08.040
We don't quite know how to deal with

01:04:12.030 --> 01:04:14.587
the richness in this statistical data

01:04:14.587 --> 01:04:16.350
with all of the parameters.

01:04:16.350 --> 01:04:17.183
Right?

01:04:17.183 --> 01:04:19.200
Doesn't GPT-3 have something like

01:04:19.200 --> 01:04:21.977
1.5 billion parameters?
- Yeah.

01:04:21.977 --> 01:04:22.972
Yes.

01:04:22.972 --> 01:04:24.120
- You know, what the hell does that mean?

01:04:24.120 --> 01:04:24.953
And--

01:04:26.274 --> 01:04:29.850
- But GPT-3 is uncanny.

01:04:29.850 --> 01:04:31.927
I went in at an exercise.

01:04:31.927 --> 01:04:33.086
- Yeah, yeah.

01:04:33.086 --> 01:04:35.190
- And because I've published
a lot that's out in the world,

01:04:35.190 --> 01:04:37.800
I said, in the style of Priya.

01:04:37.800 --> 01:04:39.302
- Right.

01:04:39.302 --> 01:04:40.430
- Argue for X.
- Yeah.

01:04:40.430 --> 01:04:41.670
- It was stunning.

01:04:41.670 --> 01:04:43.380
I was stunned.

01:04:43.380 --> 01:04:45.693
And it really could have been my words.

01:04:47.426 --> 01:04:48.259
And--
- Well,

01:04:48.259 --> 01:04:50.250
so I mean, it is stunning.

01:04:50.250 --> 01:04:54.060
I think even the conversation

01:04:54.060 --> 01:04:55.923
that was published with Lambda,

01:04:58.260 --> 01:05:00.540
this bought at Google,

01:05:00.540 --> 01:05:03.030
which the creator thought
was consciousness--

01:05:03.030 --> 01:05:05.380
Conscious and got fired
and so on and so forth.

01:05:06.330 --> 01:05:07.410
It's really stunning.

01:05:07.410 --> 01:05:09.090
I mean, if my parents were still alive,

01:05:09.090 --> 01:05:09.923
I would have them read it.

01:05:09.923 --> 01:05:13.413
It's like, oh my God,
this can in fact happen.

01:05:14.460 --> 01:05:15.293
Now...

01:05:20.310 --> 01:05:21.390
One question is, you know,

01:05:21.390 --> 01:05:22.500
what's going on with those systems?

01:05:22.500 --> 01:05:23.333
Should we trust them?

01:05:23.333 --> 01:05:24.404
And so on and so forth.

01:05:24.404 --> 01:05:25.237
Do they know what the hell
they're talking about?

01:05:25.237 --> 01:05:26.070
And stuff.

01:05:26.070 --> 01:05:26.903
That's not this talk.

01:05:26.903 --> 01:05:28.380
That's more what the promise book

01:05:28.380 --> 01:05:30.353
that I wrote a couple years ago is about.

01:05:31.804 --> 01:05:33.060
And I think the answer is no.

01:05:33.060 --> 01:05:34.990
This is where I think Gebru's

01:05:36.630 --> 01:05:40.950
notion of stochastic
parrots actually applies,

01:05:40.950 --> 01:05:43.350
which is that you've
written in law enough.

01:05:43.350 --> 01:05:45.450
I mean, the space of possibilities,

01:05:45.450 --> 01:05:48.903
of the construction of human sentences is,

01:05:49.770 --> 01:05:51.330
you know, is stupefyingly large.

01:05:51.330 --> 01:05:53.100
The number that have been
said is of measure zero

01:05:53.100 --> 01:05:54.300
compared to that,

01:05:54.300 --> 01:05:57.360
you've charted a certain
sub-region of this thing.

01:05:57.360 --> 01:05:59.520
It can have certain kinds of patterns,

01:05:59.520 --> 01:06:01.920
and these systems can
recognize them and stuff.

01:06:01.920 --> 01:06:04.830
But, I trust what you say.

01:06:04.830 --> 01:06:07.050
I'm not gonna trust, at least I well,

01:06:07.050 --> 01:06:10.320
better not trust a system
that is merely mimicking

01:06:10.320 --> 01:06:12.000
your speech patterns.

01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:13.380
You know, I wanna know
what you care about,

01:06:13.380 --> 01:06:14.213
what you stand for.

01:06:14.213 --> 01:06:16.620
What you would go and stand
in front of a bulldozer for.

01:06:16.620 --> 01:06:19.470
You know, if a building were burning down,

01:06:19.470 --> 01:06:21.090
what would you in fact do?

01:06:21.090 --> 01:06:22.950
Even if you've never
statistically run into

01:06:22.950 --> 01:06:24.063
the situation before.

01:06:25.170 --> 01:06:27.076
So, those ethical situations, I think

01:06:27.076 --> 01:06:30.720
are outside the can of these systems.

01:06:30.720 --> 01:06:32.420
But imitating the style, you know,

01:06:34.710 --> 01:06:35.670
it's damn impressive.

01:06:35.670 --> 01:06:39.300
And I think it's doing it
because it's doing inference,

01:06:39.300 --> 01:06:43.530
sort of non-conceptual
inference over these details.

01:06:43.530 --> 01:06:45.813
Because computers can
handle a lot of data.

01:06:47.190 --> 01:06:49.410
But we need to understand
that it's non-conceptual.

01:06:49.410 --> 01:06:52.500
We need to understand
what's the world like,

01:06:52.500 --> 01:06:54.870
such that it's in fact, getting this data.

01:06:54.870 --> 01:06:56.700
What was the origin of data?

01:06:56.700 --> 01:06:58.650
You know, if we've got something in there

01:06:58.650 --> 01:07:00.060
about how many young people smoke.

01:07:00.060 --> 01:07:02.850
And turns out that the data
come from American Airlines,

01:07:02.850 --> 01:07:04.110
and you're only a young person if you're--

01:07:04.110 --> 01:07:05.370
You're an adult if you're over two

01:07:05.370 --> 01:07:06.920
because you need your own seat.

01:07:08.490 --> 01:07:10.290
You know, all these questions
about the categories,

01:07:10.290 --> 01:07:12.260
the norms of the categories.

01:07:12.260 --> 01:07:16.803
That's the kind of question that I think,

01:07:19.057 --> 01:07:21.266
in a way, I'm trying to sort of suggest

01:07:21.266 --> 01:07:26.266
the intellectual arsenal of
forms of understanding we need

01:07:26.910 --> 01:07:28.800
in order to understand what it is

01:07:28.800 --> 01:07:30.990
that these systems are doing.

01:07:30.990 --> 01:07:32.520
Not just to be impressed by them,

01:07:32.520 --> 01:07:33.780
because otherwise we're gonna be

01:07:33.780 --> 01:07:35.343
too bloody impressed by them.

01:07:36.210 --> 01:07:38.640
- Yeah, I totally agree.

01:07:38.640 --> 01:07:39.480
Thank you very much.

01:07:39.480 --> 01:07:41.370
So, we have a couple of questions.

01:07:41.370 --> 01:07:44.400
The first one is from Peter Morgan.

01:07:44.400 --> 01:07:47.460
And he says, "Perhaps
a process or algorithm

01:07:47.460 --> 01:07:50.520
that renders non-conceptual life/ living

01:07:50.520 --> 01:07:52.410
into conceptual form.

01:07:52.410 --> 01:07:53.370
But I think you say

01:07:53.370 --> 01:07:55.920
one has to be careful about that process.

01:07:55.920 --> 01:07:58.650
I suppose we can have
a non-conceptual sense

01:07:58.650 --> 01:08:03.150
of how we render the algorithms
that perform the render,

01:08:03.150 --> 01:08:05.880
but can we render that algorithm itself?

01:08:05.880 --> 01:08:07.380
Is this a reasonable question,

01:08:07.380 --> 01:08:09.537
given that this leads to a recursion?"

01:08:10.890 --> 01:08:11.723
- Well...

01:08:16.020 --> 01:08:18.333
Okay, another glass of something to drink.

01:08:19.170 --> 01:08:20.790
I would need to understand
what you mean by

01:08:20.790 --> 01:08:21.960
an algorithm that renders conc--

01:08:21.960 --> 01:08:23.340
Actually, forget the
algorithm for the moment.

01:08:23.340 --> 01:08:26.040
I'd like to know what
you mean by rendering

01:08:26.040 --> 01:08:28.680
non-conceptual life and
living into conceptual form.

01:08:28.680 --> 01:08:30.820
Because that would suggest

01:08:33.660 --> 01:08:37.380
assessing a life, I guess.

01:08:37.380 --> 01:08:41.430
A life process or something of
probably ineffable richness.

01:08:41.430 --> 01:08:44.340
And in fact, abstracting it in such a way

01:08:44.340 --> 01:08:46.200
that it's fitting into a set of concepts.

01:08:46.200 --> 01:08:48.540
And I would wanna know
whose concepts those are.

01:08:48.540 --> 01:08:49.373
Are they yours?

01:08:49.373 --> 01:08:51.180
Are they the systems itself?

01:08:51.180 --> 01:08:52.650
Even if they're the systems itself,

01:08:52.650 --> 01:08:54.720
that doesn't prove that
that exhausts the life,

01:08:54.720 --> 01:08:57.213
because I think most of
our life, we, in fact,

01:08:58.980 --> 01:09:00.600
most of our thinking is in fact

01:09:00.600 --> 01:09:02.613
not conceptual in form, I think.

01:09:05.415 --> 01:09:07.315
You know, if you're driving down the--

01:09:08.460 --> 01:09:10.860
If we're driving in a car down the highway

01:09:10.860 --> 01:09:13.327
and I am driving and I say,

01:09:13.327 --> 01:09:14.880
"Actually, sorry,

01:09:14.880 --> 01:09:17.070
I've just been struck completely blind,

01:09:17.070 --> 01:09:17.903
but I'll keep driving.

01:09:17.903 --> 01:09:19.707
You just tell me what to do."

01:09:23.490 --> 01:09:25.390
You ought to be scared outta your mind

01:09:26.730 --> 01:09:30.060
because you can't say, look,
turn a little bit to the right

01:09:30.060 --> 01:09:31.710
or turn 27 degrees to the left.

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:33.420
That's not gonna actually
lead me to do the right thing.

01:09:33.420 --> 01:09:36.270
And at what speed and so on and so forth.

01:09:36.270 --> 01:09:38.920
I think most of our physical
navigation is in fact

01:09:40.110 --> 01:09:43.083
navigated in terms of sub
conceptual representations.

01:09:46.200 --> 01:09:47.033
So, I don't think you can,

01:09:47.033 --> 01:09:49.710
I don't think you can render
non-conceptual living systems

01:09:49.710 --> 01:09:50.970
into conceptual form,

01:09:50.970 --> 01:09:53.280
unless you're in an
artificial life situation

01:09:53.280 --> 01:09:54.450
or something like that.

01:09:54.450 --> 01:09:56.250
And I think that's one of the
things that makes those things

01:09:56.250 --> 01:09:59.283
inexorably indelibly artificial.

01:10:02.190 --> 01:10:07.190
And sexual representation is lossy, right?

01:10:07.680 --> 01:10:11.340
It does and it has to,

01:10:11.340 --> 01:10:14.040
and it's important because it does

01:10:14.040 --> 01:10:17.760
lose enormous amounts of a detail

01:10:17.760 --> 01:10:21.783
in order to get at higher
level regularities.

01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:24.240
The life can't, in fact,

01:10:24.240 --> 01:10:26.853
let go of these of
details or it would die.

01:10:28.620 --> 01:10:29.453
So, yeah.

01:10:29.453 --> 01:10:30.720
I just don't actually understand, I guess,

01:10:30.720 --> 01:10:33.900
what rendering a non-conceptual life

01:10:33.900 --> 01:10:35.160
into conceptual form would mean

01:10:35.160 --> 01:10:37.080
other than describing in conceptual terms.

01:10:37.080 --> 01:10:39.360
And I think it's a theorem
that if you prescribe a

01:10:39.360 --> 01:10:40.830
non-conceptual life in conceptual terms,

01:10:40.830 --> 01:10:42.773
you're gonna be missing a lot of detail.

01:10:45.780 --> 01:10:48.120
Sorry, I don't mean to be obstructionist.

01:10:48.120 --> 01:10:51.180
But I would need to know
what's going on better

01:10:51.180 --> 01:10:53.430
in order to know if we can answer it.

01:10:53.430 --> 01:10:54.263
- Yeah.

01:10:54.263 --> 01:10:57.540
So, we can move on to the
next question by June Lou.

01:10:57.540 --> 01:11:00.510
So, the implication of
this view for inference

01:11:00.510 --> 01:11:01.620
is the question,

01:11:01.620 --> 01:11:05.460
does it mean that inference
cannot be adequately

01:11:05.460 --> 01:11:07.680
licensed conceptually, that is,

01:11:07.680 --> 01:11:12.180
if P given even P and P greater than Q,

01:11:12.180 --> 01:11:15.090
entering Q is not automatic or autonomous,

01:11:15.090 --> 01:11:17.910
but rather requires
non-conceptual orientation

01:11:17.910 --> 01:11:20.520
to what P and Q are actually about.

01:11:20.520 --> 01:11:23.640
Likewise, the example of
recognizing Priya in Miami,

01:11:23.640 --> 01:11:27.150
which AI practitioners
today do call inference,

01:11:27.150 --> 01:11:30.570
seems to suggest that the
warrant of such inference

01:11:30.570 --> 01:11:32.370
is non-conceptual.

01:11:32.370 --> 01:11:34.520
With the non-conceptual
aspects, hopefully.

01:11:36.060 --> 01:11:37.040
- Well, I think...

01:11:42.270 --> 01:11:43.413
I think that,

01:11:44.700 --> 01:11:47.449
yes, so take the P and P and Q.

01:11:47.449 --> 01:11:49.260
Or implies Q.

01:11:49.260 --> 01:11:50.283
If we go syntactic.

01:11:58.080 --> 01:12:00.690
There are reasonable conditions in this.

01:12:00.690 --> 01:12:04.350
Reasonable conditions underlying
even conceptual inference

01:12:04.350 --> 01:12:08.340
of that sort, which I think
even a logician would recognize.

01:12:08.340 --> 01:12:12.060
Which in fact, limit
the application of that.

01:12:12.060 --> 01:12:13.353
So, for example,

01:12:16.860 --> 01:12:18.717
you know, Sorites paradoxes
and so on and so forth,

01:12:18.717 --> 01:12:20.130
the P is standing next to Q,

01:12:20.130 --> 01:12:23.270
then P is near q and Q
is standing next to R,

01:12:23.270 --> 01:12:24.570
then Q is near R.

01:12:24.570 --> 01:12:26.550
But if you do that for 10,000 people,

01:12:26.550 --> 01:12:30.060
then in fact, P is not near
the person who's 10,000 away

01:12:30.060 --> 01:12:30.893
and so on and so forth.

01:12:30.893 --> 01:12:34.680
So, how do you know how far these sort of

01:12:34.680 --> 01:12:36.933
partially transitive things go?

01:12:40.890 --> 01:12:43.590
You know, the applicability of, you know,

01:12:43.590 --> 01:12:44.423
somebody who's young,

01:12:44.423 --> 01:12:46.770
one year younger than them
is also young, and I mean,

01:12:46.770 --> 01:12:48.660
one year older than them is also young and

01:12:49.572 --> 01:12:50.872
all these kinds of things.

01:12:51.870 --> 01:12:54.990
It's kind of trivial for
introductory logic students

01:12:54.990 --> 01:12:58.740
to come up with examples
which violate the norms of

01:12:58.740 --> 01:12:59.573
conceptual inference.

01:12:59.573 --> 01:13:02.463
And I think what the logic
teachers typically say is, look,

01:13:04.440 --> 01:13:07.953
you have to operate within
a regimen of reasonableness.

01:13:09.090 --> 01:13:12.270
The problem is that when we
automate this stuff in systems

01:13:12.270 --> 01:13:13.590
and have, you know,

01:13:13.590 --> 01:13:15.870
databases being swallowed
up by these systems

01:13:15.870 --> 01:13:16.703
and so on and so forth,

01:13:16.703 --> 01:13:17.536
we actually don't know

01:13:17.536 --> 01:13:20.433
what the reasonable
conditions are and so on.

01:13:22.800 --> 01:13:24.210
You know, this is an ancient example,

01:13:24.210 --> 01:13:26.640
but when I was in MIT,
there was this, you know,

01:13:26.640 --> 01:13:28.680
as I said, 150 years ago.

01:13:28.680 --> 01:13:31.290
There was this system of medical diagnosis

01:13:31.290 --> 01:13:33.180
and it was asked how to cure

01:13:33.180 --> 01:13:37.200
a kidney infection and it said,

01:13:37.200 --> 01:13:38.800
take the kidney out and boil it.

01:13:39.750 --> 01:13:43.323
Now, that system lacks something.

01:13:45.146 --> 01:13:47.040
And, so people tried to
axiomatize common sense,

01:13:47.040 --> 01:13:48.960
and they've tried to
axiomatize humanize causality

01:13:48.960 --> 01:13:51.750
and they've tried to axiomatize
all these sorts of things.

01:13:51.750 --> 01:13:53.850
Axiomatization in the classical mold

01:13:53.850 --> 01:13:58.110
is simply a conceptual
rendering of what's going on.

01:13:58.110 --> 01:14:02.020
And I think the reason that
second wave AI has trounced

01:14:05.310 --> 01:14:06.510
not necessarily forever, I mean,

01:14:06.510 --> 01:14:07.500
there's a whole discussion here,

01:14:07.500 --> 01:14:10.060
but the reason that I think it's

01:14:11.130 --> 01:14:16.130
kind of tranced classical
AI is that in fact,

01:14:18.360 --> 01:14:21.670
purely conceptual
inference has proved to be

01:14:23.070 --> 01:14:27.270
inexorably brittle
because of the limitations

01:14:27.270 --> 01:14:28.833
of the conceptual categories.

01:14:29.730 --> 01:14:33.580
And the improved behavior of

01:14:34.620 --> 01:14:35.940
these statistical algorithms,

01:14:35.940 --> 01:14:39.183
I say, I think is because they
are not entirely conceptual.

01:14:40.170 --> 01:14:42.090
That doesn't mean they're
the best thing in the world,

01:14:42.090 --> 01:14:43.200
I don't think, for example,

01:14:43.200 --> 01:14:46.170
this idea of having a
system explain itself

01:14:46.170 --> 01:14:47.910
by constructing literatures,

01:14:47.910 --> 01:14:50.490
which would constrain the
categories to mean what it is that

01:14:50.490 --> 01:14:51.630
they mean in these particular things.

01:14:51.630 --> 01:14:52.500
And so on and so forth.

01:14:52.500 --> 01:14:55.620
This is miles beyond where we are now.

01:14:55.620 --> 01:14:58.923
But yes, I think there are limits to,

01:15:00.600 --> 01:15:02.000
there are limits to...

01:15:03.840 --> 01:15:05.073
To conceptuality.

01:15:06.930 --> 01:15:09.117
And I think it's important for AI to--

01:15:10.290 --> 01:15:12.810
like you're coming about
recognizing Priya in Miami

01:15:12.810 --> 01:15:14.010
as considered inference.

01:15:17.550 --> 01:15:21.000
Maybe somebody in AI would call
that statistical inference.

01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:21.833
- Yeah.

01:15:26.490 --> 01:15:29.190
- What can we trust
statistical inference for?

01:15:29.190 --> 01:15:34.140
That's a pretty core screened label

01:15:34.140 --> 01:15:36.990
of what's going on, I think.

01:15:36.990 --> 01:15:40.950
And I think the same issues
about statistical inference,

01:15:40.950 --> 01:15:43.290
I mean, the fact that it's
based on this idea of data

01:15:43.290 --> 01:15:46.050
where the data are
these corpuscular things

01:15:46.050 --> 01:15:47.406
where the assumption is that the

01:15:47.406 --> 01:15:49.560
instantiation of the properties
across lots of different

01:15:49.560 --> 01:15:51.180
objects and lots of
different circumstances,

01:15:51.180 --> 01:15:53.340
it's the same property
and so on and so forth.

01:15:53.340 --> 01:15:55.416
I think there're,

01:15:55.416 --> 01:15:57.510
you know, it's an advance over GOFI.

01:15:57.510 --> 01:16:00.723
But I think lots of these
issues of homogeneity,

01:16:03.000 --> 01:16:05.550
not knowing the norms that
underlie the use of the

01:16:05.550 --> 01:16:06.570
categories and so on and so forth.

01:16:06.570 --> 01:16:08.730
They're gonna, they're gonna defeat,

01:16:08.730 --> 01:16:11.460
I mean, uncannily good as they look,

01:16:11.460 --> 01:16:12.910
I think they're gonna defeat.

01:16:15.330 --> 01:16:16.950
They're gonna defeat even this,

01:16:16.950 --> 01:16:18.228
even second wave inference.

01:16:18.228 --> 01:16:19.680
I think it, you know, it will be looked at

01:16:19.680 --> 01:16:22.923
as narrow as first wave AI
inference is looked at now.

01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:26.160
- Thank you.

01:16:26.160 --> 01:16:28.477
We now have a question
from Sylvia who asked,

01:16:28.477 --> 01:16:30.810
"Regarding the precision
of athletic decisions

01:16:30.810 --> 01:16:33.480
involved in navigating whitewater rapids.

01:16:33.480 --> 01:16:36.480
I think you were saying that
because we cannot conceptualize

01:16:36.480 --> 01:16:39.750
the pools than rocks and
eddies around a bend,

01:16:39.750 --> 01:16:43.350
notice that we nevertheless
steer and survive

01:16:43.350 --> 01:16:45.420
without one causal inference.

01:16:45.420 --> 01:16:48.630
Non-conceptual inference
takes the limitations of the

01:16:48.630 --> 01:16:51.360
programmers coding out of the equation.

01:16:51.360 --> 01:16:53.520
I like that, but I'm not a mathematician.

01:16:53.520 --> 01:16:55.530
How would AI plant a garden?"

01:16:55.530 --> 01:16:56.780
Oh, I love that question.

01:17:03.210 --> 01:17:04.370
- Well, first of all...

01:17:07.830 --> 01:17:08.730
Yeah, how would AI?

01:17:08.730 --> 01:17:11.700
I mean, I don't think the
word AI has a stable enough

01:17:11.700 --> 01:17:14.040
reference to enable me to
answer the last question.

01:17:14.040 --> 01:17:18.397
Although my initial gut level
response was sitting down.

01:17:18.397 --> 01:17:22.380
(Priya chuckles softly)

01:17:22.380 --> 01:17:24.090
But the first thing about, you know,

01:17:24.090 --> 01:17:29.090
the skill of going through this whitewater

01:17:29.370 --> 01:17:30.920
with that one causal inference,

01:17:32.430 --> 01:17:35.010
I think this goes back to Priya's point.

01:17:35.010 --> 01:17:37.680
It isn't one causal inference
in the sense that inference

01:17:37.680 --> 01:17:40.830
was classically thought about
in classical logical terms

01:17:40.830 --> 01:17:42.270
and AI terms.

01:17:42.270 --> 01:17:46.320
It probably is an inference
in the sense that June--

01:17:46.320 --> 01:17:49.440
Genoa was just mentioning
that contemporary statistical

01:17:49.440 --> 01:17:52.503
AI would actually take it
to be causal inference,

01:17:56.190 --> 01:17:59.973
quite possibly continuous, you know,

01:18:00.990 --> 01:18:03.270
dynamic interaction with an environment

01:18:03.270 --> 01:18:06.330
in ways that honored causal consequences

01:18:06.330 --> 01:18:07.480
and so on and so forth.

01:18:13.290 --> 01:18:15.810
I kind of don't doubt
that you could build an AI

01:18:15.810 --> 01:18:19.650
even kind of at the moment
that could manage a canoe

01:18:19.650 --> 01:18:21.584
down some of these rivers.

01:18:21.584 --> 01:18:24.033
I mean, I'm no expert,
I'm not a programmer.

01:18:26.040 --> 01:18:27.900
So, I think we have to know better

01:18:27.900 --> 01:18:29.373
what causal inference means.

01:18:30.420 --> 01:18:33.270
I mean, Priya was laughing at me, I hope,

01:18:33.270 --> 01:18:36.030
with respect to my needing
words well defined,

01:18:36.030 --> 01:18:37.680
especially since I don't
believe in definitions.

01:18:37.680 --> 01:18:41.277
But the issue of what the word mean,

01:18:41.277 --> 01:18:43.380
it, you know, matters with
respect to this stuff.

01:18:43.380 --> 01:18:45.120
Because in fact, by that
one causal inference,

01:18:45.120 --> 01:18:46.830
we need to know what inference is such

01:18:46.830 --> 01:18:47.940
that it doesn't involve it.

01:18:47.940 --> 01:18:50.070
And we need to know what
inference might mean in the

01:18:50.070 --> 01:18:52.350
statistical sense and so on and so forth.

01:18:52.350 --> 01:18:56.070
In order to know what form of
attunement to the causality

01:18:56.070 --> 01:18:57.420
structure of the world,

01:18:57.420 --> 01:18:59.790
the systems are actually capable of.

01:18:59.790 --> 01:19:01.530
Non-conceptual interest
takes the limitations

01:19:01.530 --> 01:19:03.580
the program is coding outta the equation.

01:19:04.500 --> 01:19:05.973
You know, that's very tricky.

01:19:07.110 --> 01:19:10.170
I just taught this course on ethics of AI,

01:19:10.170 --> 01:19:12.870
and this issue came up as
to whether the programmers

01:19:12.870 --> 01:19:14.100
are in fact, culpable at all

01:19:14.100 --> 01:19:16.443
for the results they come up with.

01:19:18.694 --> 01:19:20.160
And some of the people
who actually work with

01:19:20.160 --> 01:19:21.510
contemporary AI systems say,

01:19:21.510 --> 01:19:22.500
look, the programmers do nothing.

01:19:22.500 --> 01:19:24.090
They just programing causal, you know,

01:19:24.090 --> 01:19:28.380
gradient descent among
huge regions of, you know,

01:19:28.380 --> 01:19:30.090
spaces of continuous variable.

01:19:30.090 --> 01:19:32.760
And in fact, programming causal
descent among them is not,

01:19:32.760 --> 01:19:37.760
doesn't take genius even if
it pays you a lot of money.

01:19:40.530 --> 01:19:42.610
But there are issues about

01:19:45.510 --> 01:19:48.600
what data you're gathering
and so on and so forth,

01:19:48.600 --> 01:19:49.590
which are pretty tricky.

01:19:49.590 --> 01:19:51.330
I mean, if you have,

01:19:51.330 --> 01:19:52.580
you know, a sex...

01:19:56.820 --> 01:20:00.060
You know, an entry for sex or something

01:20:00.060 --> 01:20:01.050
in a database and stuff,

01:20:01.050 --> 01:20:02.430
you know, are you gonna
have sex and gender?

01:20:02.430 --> 01:20:03.520
Or sex and--

01:20:03.520 --> 01:20:04.710
Is it gonna be separate?

01:20:04.710 --> 01:20:06.180
And sex, is it gonna be male or female?

01:20:06.180 --> 01:20:08.490
Or is it gonna be XX, or XY,

01:20:08.490 --> 01:20:11.673
or XYY, or XXY, or XO?

01:20:13.290 --> 01:20:15.810
What about people who are
XY but don't have androgen

01:20:15.810 --> 01:20:18.360
receptors and so they grow
up phenotypically female

01:20:18.360 --> 01:20:20.160
and they don't actually
realize that they're XY

01:20:20.160 --> 01:20:24.566
until they possibly get tests
for infertility later on

01:20:24.566 --> 01:20:25.399
and so on and so forth.

01:20:25.399 --> 01:20:27.060
Then it can be quite
shocking for them to discover

01:20:27.060 --> 01:20:28.290
that they've been XY their whole life

01:20:28.290 --> 01:20:29.310
and so on and so forth.

01:20:29.310 --> 01:20:31.804
These categories are not innocent.

01:20:31.804 --> 01:20:34.799
And you know,

01:20:34.799 --> 01:20:37.620
and race of course is an
absolutely non-innocent category.

01:20:37.620 --> 01:20:38.453
When I was a kid,

01:20:38.453 --> 01:20:39.780
I asked my parents why--

01:20:39.780 --> 01:20:41.190
I was like six or something,

01:20:41.190 --> 01:20:44.100
why if a black person and
a white person had a kid,

01:20:44.100 --> 01:20:48.120
the kid wasn't splotchy like a Jersey cow

01:20:48.120 --> 01:20:49.948
or something like this.

01:20:49.948 --> 01:20:51.498
And you know, I got laughed at.

01:20:53.021 --> 01:20:54.960
But, I think the answer was because

01:20:54.960 --> 01:20:58.850
race is not a biological
category, you know, and that's...

01:21:03.300 --> 01:21:05.310
The can categories aren't innocent.

01:21:05.310 --> 01:21:07.530
That's what feminist epistemologists,

01:21:07.530 --> 01:21:09.150
and feminist scientists
and so on and so forth,

01:21:09.150 --> 01:21:11.970
they've been arguing for so long.

01:21:11.970 --> 01:21:13.803
These categories are inflected.

01:21:14.700 --> 01:21:17.400
And partly what I'm trying
to do is to, I mean,

01:21:17.400 --> 01:21:18.850
I dunno if this comes across,

01:21:20.130 --> 01:21:23.580
I wanna do justice to that recognition

01:21:23.580 --> 01:21:25.962
from the science studies
and feminist methodologist

01:21:25.962 --> 01:21:27.060
and so on and so forth.

01:21:27.060 --> 01:21:29.757
Even though I'm resolutely realist,

01:21:35.520 --> 01:21:39.720
because I actually think this
stupifying surpassing detail

01:21:39.720 --> 01:21:41.733
of the world is actually the same.

01:21:43.110 --> 01:21:46.230
And if I and someone else
register it in different ways,

01:21:46.230 --> 01:21:50.910
it's the same detail that were in fact

01:21:50.910 --> 01:21:53.580
abstracting in various ways
that !suit our particular

01:21:53.580 --> 01:21:56.850
cultural assumptions and norms
and so on and so forth. So.

01:21:56.850 --> 01:21:58.156
- And Brian is this,

01:21:58.156 --> 01:22:00.720
is this not much more salient
when we are thinking of like

01:22:00.720 --> 01:22:02.790
standard, you know,

01:22:02.790 --> 01:22:05.940
bottom of the barrel machine
learning tasks, for example,

01:22:05.940 --> 01:22:07.890
where we provide a training set.

01:22:07.890 --> 01:22:11.160
So, the choice of training set,

01:22:11.160 --> 01:22:13.260
not only what data we collect,

01:22:13.260 --> 01:22:15.633
but what we choose as the training.

01:22:16.568 --> 01:22:17.606
- Well, right.

01:22:17.606 --> 01:22:18.720
And how the,

01:22:18.720 --> 01:22:22.470
how the training set was
gathered and on and on and on.

01:22:22.470 --> 01:22:23.490
Yeah.

01:22:23.490 --> 01:22:26.090
This is, um...

01:22:28.080 --> 01:22:29.073
Yes, absolutely.

01:22:31.680 --> 01:22:36.030
- Okay, so, Peter has another
quick comment and a question.

01:22:36.030 --> 01:22:38.970
You mentioned that there is
a lossiness in how we think

01:22:38.970 --> 01:22:39.803
about life.

01:22:41.206 --> 01:22:43.440
Thought is a compression is his question.

01:22:43.440 --> 01:22:47.656
- Well, yes, I think it's
a compression in a way.

01:22:47.656 --> 01:22:49.290
I mean, it's tricky cause
the word compression

01:22:49.290 --> 01:22:50.340
comes from information theory.

01:22:50.340 --> 01:22:54.506
And the idea is that there's
information there and you know,

01:22:54.506 --> 01:22:56.310
and the measures of information,

01:22:56.310 --> 01:22:58.198
in fact buy into these
conceptual assumptions

01:22:58.198 --> 01:22:59.031
and so on and so forth.

01:22:59.031 --> 01:22:59.864
And so in lots of ways.

01:22:59.864 --> 01:23:04.864
So, there's problems with circularity

01:23:05.100 --> 01:23:06.800
in the foundation of the question.

01:23:10.440 --> 01:23:13.440
I do think that thought
is lossy to some extent,

01:23:13.440 --> 01:23:14.720
but I think that...

01:23:17.550 --> 01:23:20.220
Remember I said we live
in this middle distance

01:23:20.220 --> 01:23:23.520
between the detail and the
abstractions and stuff,

01:23:23.520 --> 01:23:26.280
and we navigate them and we
take ethical responsibility,

01:23:26.280 --> 01:23:29.040
moral responsibility for the abstractions.

01:23:29.040 --> 01:23:31.470
We have to, I mean, we should anyway,

01:23:31.470 --> 01:23:33.450
and navigating them
and so on and so forth,

01:23:33.450 --> 01:23:36.757
and understanding how they would
go into practice and stuff.

01:23:36.757 --> 01:23:38.520
And so if I say to a surgeon,

01:23:38.520 --> 01:23:39.680
look, you've got to...

01:23:43.050 --> 01:23:44.700
Oh and here's a real
example from the beginning

01:23:44.700 --> 01:23:48.810
of Xerox Park when we first
made Windows and mice.

01:23:48.810 --> 01:23:49.643
And the question was,

01:23:49.643 --> 01:23:52.380
how fast the mouse should
track the, you know,

01:23:52.380 --> 01:23:54.183
the cursor should track the mouse.

01:23:55.170 --> 01:23:57.368
And somebody wrote up
a paper saying it was,

01:23:57.368 --> 01:23:59.318
I don't know, whatever, 3.5 inches were

01:24:01.770 --> 01:24:06.070
on the screen compared to an inch on the

01:24:07.260 --> 01:24:08.490
pad or something like that.

01:24:08.490 --> 01:24:09.810
And it turns out that's not what matters.

01:24:09.810 --> 01:24:14.130
What matters is you wanna be
able to cross the whole screen

01:24:14.130 --> 01:24:15.930
with a single rotation of the wrist.

01:24:23.520 --> 01:24:24.353
The...

01:24:27.660 --> 01:24:29.353
Well, I guess the point is it,

01:24:30.219 --> 01:24:31.140
it it sort of the same one

01:24:31.140 --> 01:24:34.270
that there's assumptions in the categories

01:24:37.320 --> 01:24:40.920
in terms of which we construct

01:24:40.920 --> 01:24:43.320
these non-conceptual representations
and so on and so forth

01:24:43.320 --> 01:24:45.820
that we as programmers
kind of need to take

01:24:48.150 --> 01:24:49.653
responsibility for.

01:24:51.990 --> 01:24:53.850
And if things are, as Priya says,

01:24:53.850 --> 01:24:56.730
and in fact a lot of the
problems are in how the data's

01:24:56.730 --> 01:24:59.153
collected and what the data
assumes and so on and so forth.

01:25:00.319 --> 01:25:02.790
The, you know, and students I had

01:25:02.790 --> 01:25:04.170
in class this week,

01:25:04.170 --> 01:25:06.960
this semester didn't think
the systems were capable

01:25:06.960 --> 01:25:08.373
of taking ethical responsibility at all.

01:25:08.373 --> 01:25:10.710
They didn't even occur to them
because they don't have this

01:25:10.710 --> 01:25:13.923
model of AI that we had four years ago.

01:25:15.360 --> 01:25:16.193
So, it's a mess.

01:25:16.193 --> 01:25:17.543
It's a mess.

01:25:17.543 --> 01:25:19.470
I mean, I would say yes,

01:25:19.470 --> 01:25:23.883
thinking is loses a lot.

01:25:25.380 --> 01:25:30.380
But good thinkers know how to
take responsibility for that,

01:25:31.463 --> 01:25:32.673
which the thoughts lose.

01:25:35.430 --> 01:25:36.450
- That's a great answer.

01:25:36.450 --> 01:25:37.290
Thank you.

01:25:37.290 --> 01:25:38.857
So, Gary has the next question.

01:25:38.857 --> 01:25:40.800
"What is the implication of scale?

01:25:40.800 --> 01:25:43.887
For example, a concept
of a person is fuzzy,

01:25:43.887 --> 01:25:46.200
but what about the concept of a photon

01:25:46.200 --> 01:25:47.970
or a gravitational field?

01:25:47.970 --> 01:25:49.500
A star is a fuzzy concept,

01:25:49.500 --> 01:25:53.007
but the feels created
by the star is precise."

01:25:53.910 --> 01:25:55.610
- Well, okay, so here's a problem,

01:25:57.000 --> 01:25:57.870
possibly with me.

01:25:57.870 --> 01:26:00.270
I have never understood what people say

01:26:00.270 --> 01:26:02.133
when they say concepts are fuzzy.

01:26:03.300 --> 01:26:05.043
I may just lack the right gene.

01:26:07.020 --> 01:26:10.667
But, I've had arguments with this with,

01:26:10.667 --> 01:26:14.149
I remember John Holland and John McDowell

01:26:14.149 --> 01:26:18.063
and a bunch of other people
at Chicago at the time.

01:26:22.590 --> 01:26:25.050
Is it that, for example,
a person being fuzzy,

01:26:25.050 --> 01:26:27.940
does it mean that there are
edge cases which we don't

01:26:30.330 --> 01:26:31.533
know how to judge?

01:26:33.180 --> 01:26:36.120
Does it mean their individual
edge cases are hard to judge,

01:26:36.120 --> 01:26:38.240
but that we don't actually...

01:26:43.380 --> 01:26:44.970
I mean one of these are,

01:26:44.970 --> 01:26:47.250
we don't know how to define
it even though in a particular

01:26:47.250 --> 01:26:49.563
case we would know how to use it.

01:26:51.480 --> 01:26:52.630
Maybe we don't have to,

01:26:53.700 --> 01:26:55.832
don't know how to apply
it to detail like bread.

01:26:55.832 --> 01:26:57.960
You know, let's say we have, let's say,

01:26:57.960 --> 01:27:00.110
let's have the opposite
of bread for supper

01:27:00.949 --> 01:27:01.782
and somebody can say,

01:27:01.782 --> 01:27:02.940
how can we have the
opposite of bread, right?

01:27:02.940 --> 01:27:04.080
So, what's bread?

01:27:04.080 --> 01:27:05.100
Is it hot cross buns?

01:27:05.100 --> 01:27:05.933
Is it bagels?

01:27:05.933 --> 01:27:06.766
Is it latkes?

01:27:06.766 --> 01:27:09.330
Is it, you know, naan or chapatis?

01:27:09.330 --> 01:27:11.310
Or I mean, God knows what.

01:27:14.003 --> 01:27:15.723
But, is that fuzzy?

01:27:17.280 --> 01:27:18.510
Like, I'm not sure it's fuzzy,

01:27:18.510 --> 01:27:20.340
it's just not clearly applicable.

01:27:20.340 --> 01:27:22.799
And it's grab on reality is maybe,

01:27:22.799 --> 01:27:26.343
you know, looser.

01:27:30.570 --> 01:27:31.650
Stars of fuzzy concept,

01:27:31.650 --> 01:27:33.340
I guess you mean by that

01:27:38.370 --> 01:27:42.467
maybe what kinds of fusion
you need in a system.

01:27:44.760 --> 01:27:45.960
You know, there could be

01:27:47.820 --> 01:27:49.500
levels of fusion which don't raise it to

01:27:49.500 --> 01:27:51.270
actually being a star
in this kind of stuff.

01:27:51.270 --> 01:27:54.780
Maybe there are conjoined star systems

01:27:54.780 --> 01:27:56.880
which are so close that
it's not actually clear

01:27:56.880 --> 01:27:57.840
if it's one star or two.

01:27:57.840 --> 01:27:59.313
That would be a better case.

01:28:00.240 --> 01:28:01.890
The fields created by the star is precise.

01:28:01.890 --> 01:28:03.640
That's an interesting point because

01:28:07.980 --> 01:28:10.080
one of the things that I think is true

01:28:10.080 --> 01:28:11.880
about computer science

01:28:11.880 --> 01:28:13.800
and to some extent by physics too,

01:28:13.800 --> 01:28:18.070
is that we are used to
dealing with systems at

01:28:19.860 --> 01:28:22.803
an enormously wide variety of scales.

01:28:24.570 --> 01:28:26.490
You know, I mean I think computer science,

01:28:26.490 --> 01:28:31.490
it regularly crosses like 15
orders of magnitude of scale

01:28:33.249 --> 01:28:35.040
from picosecond junction transit times

01:28:35.040 --> 01:28:37.971
to running times of Ethereum,

01:28:37.971 --> 01:28:42.397
mining systems and so on and so forth.

01:28:43.358 --> 01:28:45.870
And some of the categories
that apply at some levels

01:28:45.870 --> 01:28:46.830
don't apply at others.

01:28:46.830 --> 01:28:50.670
So for example, it might
be that the features

01:28:50.670 --> 01:28:51.510
or the properties,

01:28:51.510 --> 01:28:53.430
I think they might be
features that in fact

01:28:53.430 --> 01:28:57.540
have precise boundaries
at very low levels.

01:28:57.540 --> 01:28:59.280
When you put them together
in this complexity,

01:28:59.280 --> 01:29:02.190
you end up at high levels
without there being completely

01:29:02.190 --> 01:29:03.483
precise, you know.

01:29:04.350 --> 01:29:07.670
So, taken being an...

01:29:10.110 --> 01:29:12.213
An implementation of PDF reading,

01:29:13.230 --> 01:29:15.720
that's surely fuzzy in some
sense in the PDF standard

01:29:15.720 --> 01:29:18.060
is nobody actually
implements it completely.

01:29:18.060 --> 01:29:19.980
And you're gonna have
things maybe for Microsoft,

01:29:19.980 --> 01:29:21.720
which in fact do a reasonable job,

01:29:21.720 --> 01:29:23.790
but in fact violate lots of cases.

01:29:23.790 --> 01:29:26.130
And maybe they accept cases
which are not part of the PDF

01:29:26.130 --> 01:29:28.650
but then practice goes
with what Microsoft does

01:29:28.650 --> 01:29:30.210
rather than specification.

01:29:30.210 --> 01:29:31.350
And then it becomes true

01:29:31.350 --> 01:29:33.270
that if you write it to the specification,

01:29:33.270 --> 01:29:36.330
it may not be accepted
as a PDF by Microsoft.

01:29:36.330 --> 01:29:37.163
You know?

01:29:37.163 --> 01:29:39.010
Or by Adobe Acrobat or whatever it is.

01:29:42.030 --> 01:29:45.180
What happens to precision as you cross

01:29:45.180 --> 01:29:47.493
scale boundaries is,

01:29:48.450 --> 01:29:51.240
well, I think that ought to
be taught in high school.

01:29:51.240 --> 01:29:53.733
I think these issues of scale,

01:29:56.490 --> 01:30:01.490
how to think with scale
are hugely important.

01:30:02.070 --> 01:30:06.210
And I mean the implication of scale is

01:30:06.210 --> 01:30:07.710
we should think hard about it,

01:30:07.710 --> 01:30:10.350
and think about what properties
cross scale boundaries

01:30:10.350 --> 01:30:11.433
and which ones don't.

01:30:14.160 --> 01:30:15.310
Maybe that's my answer.

01:30:16.560 --> 01:30:18.600
Leave the fuzziness aside.

01:30:18.600 --> 01:30:19.433
- Right.

01:30:19.433 --> 01:30:20.850
So, I think at this time,

01:30:20.850 --> 01:30:23.160
we probably have time for
just one more question

01:30:23.160 --> 01:30:25.620
before we wrap up and if Brian is willing,

01:30:25.620 --> 01:30:28.140
people can stay on a little bit after

01:30:28.140 --> 01:30:31.080
officially to continue the Q&amp;A.

01:30:31.080 --> 01:30:33.097
So, we have this question
from Neils who says,

01:30:33.097 --> 01:30:36.820
"Your examples seem to be
only on our perception and or

01:30:38.280 --> 01:30:40.140
conception of the physical world.

01:30:40.140 --> 01:30:43.110
Would your analysis also be
applicable to the social world

01:30:43.110 --> 01:30:46.950
in concepts like democracy
or justice and social roles,

01:30:46.950 --> 01:30:48.210
like being a judge?

01:30:48.210 --> 01:30:51.060
A person either is or is not a judge,

01:30:51.060 --> 01:30:54.930
which to me seems a bit
different from the ridge example.

01:30:54.930 --> 01:30:56.370
Do you agree and if you do,

01:30:56.370 --> 01:30:58.860
what are the consequences
for the generality

01:30:58.860 --> 01:30:59.880
of your arguments?

01:30:59.880 --> 01:31:00.870
Oof.

01:31:00.870 --> 01:31:01.703
Big question.

01:31:01.703 --> 01:31:02.536
- Well...

01:31:03.600 --> 01:31:05.514
Yeah, fortunately I know Neils.

01:31:05.514 --> 01:31:08.100
(Priya chuckles)

01:31:08.100 --> 01:31:09.633
I would say they do apply.

01:31:10.620 --> 01:31:12.600
I think the ca--

01:31:12.600 --> 01:31:14.460
I mean this goes back to
Priya's question early on

01:31:14.460 --> 01:31:16.380
about, look, we have to
have a social agreement

01:31:16.380 --> 01:31:18.900
or even determination
of hack with respect to

01:31:18.900 --> 01:31:20.310
category boundaries.

01:31:20.310 --> 01:31:21.957
I think category boundaries is--

01:31:24.750 --> 01:31:25.980
If you'll forgive me in analogy,

01:31:25.980 --> 01:31:27.360
I kind of think of categories as,

01:31:27.360 --> 01:31:29.520
as in the classical model
as being viewed at like

01:31:29.520 --> 01:31:31.950
square waves in the following sense.

01:31:31.950 --> 01:31:34.200
They are thought to have sharp boundaries

01:31:34.200 --> 01:31:35.580
like the edges of the square.

01:31:35.580 --> 01:31:37.230
So something either is or is not a judge,

01:31:37.230 --> 01:31:38.400
as you say.

01:31:38.400 --> 01:31:41.371
And then they're thought to
have flat tops in the sense that

01:31:41.371 --> 01:31:45.420
the being a judgenness of
a given judge is the same

01:31:45.420 --> 01:31:48.300
being a judgeness as someone else.

01:31:48.300 --> 01:31:49.890
And I'm not sure that's right.

01:31:49.890 --> 01:31:51.000
And I think that's one of the

01:31:51.000 --> 01:31:54.930
sort of assumptions that is so deep

01:31:54.930 --> 01:31:56.460
that I find it very difficult to actually

01:31:56.460 --> 01:31:57.870
talk to students about
what it would be for it

01:31:57.870 --> 01:31:59.610
not to be the case.

01:31:59.610 --> 01:32:01.710
But whether a person is or is not a judge,

01:32:04.110 --> 01:32:06.150
I would doubt that that's so clear.

01:32:06.150 --> 01:32:10.070
I mean, suppose...

01:32:14.700 --> 01:32:15.533
Oh, I don't know,

01:32:15.533 --> 01:32:18.550
suppose I'm a devout
Catholic and I wanna actually

01:32:19.410 --> 01:32:22.080
be married and I've been
hit by a car in an accident

01:32:22.080 --> 01:32:23.787
and I've only got a few
hours to live and stuff.

01:32:23.787 --> 01:32:26.070
And I just want a judge
to officiate our marriage

01:32:26.070 --> 01:32:28.110
so that I can be at peace,

01:32:28.110 --> 01:32:30.120
and feel like I won't go to
hell or something like this.

01:32:30.120 --> 01:32:33.540
I'm sorry if I'm stepping all
over 86,000 toes doing this

01:32:33.540 --> 01:32:34.373
example, but.

01:32:35.430 --> 01:32:36.720
And somebody says, well
look, I was a judge.

01:32:36.720 --> 01:32:37.553
I actually--

01:32:37.553 --> 01:32:40.680
My license expired last week.

01:32:40.680 --> 01:32:41.640
And I say, look, that's all right.

01:32:41.640 --> 01:32:43.050
I don't care care about that.

01:32:43.050 --> 01:32:43.883
Right?

01:32:47.250 --> 01:32:48.990
My partner and I actually
don't even agree on

01:32:48.990 --> 01:32:50.040
whether we're married

01:32:51.000 --> 01:32:53.910
because we had a ceremony
with her 95 year old father

01:32:53.910 --> 01:32:56.730
at the dining room table the
way that was classically done

01:32:56.730 --> 01:33:00.000
in ancient times and we exchanged rings

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:01.830
and we had promises
and so on and so forth.

01:33:01.830 --> 01:33:04.350
And it was wonderful cause at
the end of it he stood up and

01:33:04.350 --> 01:33:06.300
raised a glass and said,

01:33:06.300 --> 01:33:07.950
I'm so glad Brian, you've done this

01:33:07.950 --> 01:33:10.260
cause I've always wondered
about your intentions.

01:33:10.260 --> 01:33:12.630
And, (scoffs) you know,

01:33:12.630 --> 01:33:15.450
only somebody at 95 could say
that with a smile, I think,

01:33:15.450 --> 01:33:18.527
and mean it earnestly
and so on and so forth.

01:33:18.527 --> 01:33:21.180
And to some extent that strikes me as

01:33:21.180 --> 01:33:23.340
the authentic marriage.

01:33:23.340 --> 01:33:24.520
It just wasn't the one

01:33:27.840 --> 01:33:28.770
recognized by the state.

01:33:28.770 --> 01:33:30.690
Except in Canada, it is
recognized by the state

01:33:30.690 --> 01:33:31.523
and so on and so forth.

01:33:31.523 --> 01:33:36.480
So, you know,

01:33:36.480 --> 01:33:37.800
whether someone's a judge or not,

01:33:37.800 --> 01:33:40.030
I mean, if you wanna reduce things to

01:33:42.360 --> 01:33:47.360
the adequacy of formal documents,

01:33:50.040 --> 01:33:54.243
then you get to what
Nunberg was talking about

01:33:54.243 --> 01:33:56.760
with the rise of print and the sort of

01:33:56.760 --> 01:33:58.770
corpuscularization of data.

01:33:58.770 --> 01:34:02.967
And, you know, that--

01:34:04.320 --> 01:34:07.827
The digital identity criteria
of print has in fact--

01:34:09.570 --> 01:34:11.580
First of all recorded social facts,

01:34:11.580 --> 01:34:15.240
but then actually become used
as in fact it determinates of

01:34:15.240 --> 01:34:17.580
the boundaries of these categories.

01:34:17.580 --> 01:34:18.590
And I'm...

01:34:21.450 --> 01:34:22.650
So, I actually,

01:34:22.650 --> 01:34:26.490
with with enormous respect
and with an amount of respect

01:34:26.490 --> 01:34:29.460
that is not itself formally measurable,

01:34:29.460 --> 01:34:32.010
I would differ with whether in fact

01:34:32.010 --> 01:34:33.663
judges are or not judges.

01:34:35.768 --> 01:34:36.601
- Okay.

01:34:36.601 --> 01:34:39.630
So, I'm really tempted to
have one final question

01:34:39.630 --> 01:34:44.097
included Brian, and
that's from Steven Hothma.

01:34:44.097 --> 01:34:45.175
- Okay.

01:34:45.175 --> 01:34:46.008
- "Great talk.

01:34:46.008 --> 01:34:49.350
Near the end, you made the
point that these are not

01:34:49.350 --> 01:34:53.970
quite your words," as he says,
"representing which detail

01:34:53.970 --> 01:34:58.970
is good for action, but bad
long distance inference.

01:34:59.760 --> 01:35:01.470
But as your examples show,

01:35:01.470 --> 01:35:04.470
inference requires making the normatively

01:35:04.470 --> 01:35:07.380
right abstractions that retain the details

01:35:07.380 --> 01:35:09.600
that matter to the context.

01:35:09.600 --> 01:35:10.740
This is non-trivial.

01:35:10.740 --> 01:35:13.260
And I assume you would say
that the skill of making

01:35:13.260 --> 01:35:15.210
context appropriate abstractions

01:35:15.210 --> 01:35:18.120
is a form of a non-conceptual skill.

01:35:18.120 --> 01:35:18.960
But if so,

01:35:18.960 --> 01:35:22.470
does it mean that we'll never
be able to explain the success

01:35:22.470 --> 01:35:26.040
of a generalized AI if we somehow manage

01:35:26.040 --> 01:35:27.900
to achieve such a thing?"

01:35:27.900 --> 01:35:29.370
- Well, great question.

01:35:29.370 --> 01:35:30.220
Thank you, Steve.

01:35:31.770 --> 01:35:34.830
I think that yes,

01:35:34.830 --> 01:35:39.270
managing the loss in a way
that doesn't do violence

01:35:39.270 --> 01:35:43.870
to what matters about the situation

01:35:45.390 --> 01:35:47.880
is absolutely part of wisdom

01:35:47.880 --> 01:35:50.013
and a part of being an adult, basically.

01:35:52.140 --> 01:35:55.090
I have an enormous respect for that.

01:35:55.090 --> 01:35:57.270
And I a hundred percent
agree that it's non-trivial.

01:35:57.270 --> 01:36:00.150
And I think one of the things
that scares the wits outta me

01:36:00.150 --> 01:36:05.150
is that we will not only
model our AI systems

01:36:05.250 --> 01:36:08.130
on essentially trivial models
of judgment and inference.

01:36:08.130 --> 01:36:10.410
But, then we will raise our
children to think that in fact,

01:36:10.410 --> 01:36:12.840
doing judgment the way the
systems do is constitutes

01:36:12.840 --> 01:36:16.260
being an adult and
we're gonna actually all

01:36:16.260 --> 01:36:17.130
go down the drain

01:36:17.130 --> 01:36:20.970
because of our conception of humanity

01:36:20.970 --> 01:36:23.040
has been torqued by our
attempt ability systems.

01:36:23.040 --> 01:36:24.930
And we're gonna lose exactly
these non-trivial things.

01:36:24.930 --> 01:36:27.527
So yes, a hundred percent
see you and raise you one

01:36:27.527 --> 01:36:29.670
on the non-triviality.

01:36:29.670 --> 01:36:31.410
Whether it's a form of
non-conceptual skill,

01:36:31.410 --> 01:36:33.900
I actually think it probably is.

01:36:33.900 --> 01:36:36.177
However, your last sentence I have to--

01:36:37.710 --> 01:36:40.380
I had people worked in
my group at Xerox Park,

01:36:40.380 --> 01:36:42.180
40 years ago or whatever,

01:36:42.180 --> 01:36:44.190
were gonna get me vanity license plates

01:36:44.190 --> 01:36:45.023
when they first came out.

01:36:45.023 --> 01:36:46.657
And the vanity license plate
they were gonna get me was,

01:36:46.657 --> 01:36:47.943
"YES...BETBUT"

01:36:50.910 --> 01:36:53.360
Which I'm afraid is actually
sort of appropriate.

01:36:55.890 --> 01:36:57.630
Does this mean we'll
never be able to explain

01:36:57.630 --> 01:36:59.613
the assess of a general AI?

01:37:01.650 --> 01:37:02.483
First of all,

01:37:02.483 --> 01:37:04.380
I don't think we're gonna get a general AI

01:37:04.380 --> 01:37:07.890
if it in fact, buys into the current idea

01:37:07.890 --> 01:37:10.503
of explanation being entirely conceptual.

01:37:11.520 --> 01:37:15.810
If being conceptual is taken
to be this particular model

01:37:15.810 --> 01:37:18.963
of conceptual that I've talked about here.

01:37:22.710 --> 01:37:25.080
The thing I was trying to
say in my epilogue comment

01:37:25.080 --> 01:37:26.457
about explainability is--

01:37:27.930 --> 01:37:29.700
And actually, I mean throughout the talk,

01:37:29.700 --> 01:37:31.140
I didn't make a big deal of it,

01:37:31.140 --> 01:37:33.590
but anyone who is alert
to it, will have noticed.

01:37:34.920 --> 01:37:38.850
I don't think the use of words
is intrinsically conceptual

01:37:38.850 --> 01:37:41.400
in the sense that I've
characterized conceptuality.

01:37:43.017 --> 01:37:46.590
I think poets and novelists,

01:37:46.590 --> 01:37:47.423
and not only that,

01:37:47.423 --> 01:37:49.110
but I think great intellectualism,
so on and so forth,

01:37:49.110 --> 01:37:51.633
use words in ways that outscript

01:37:54.000 --> 01:37:57.780
the constrictions on
conceptuality that I've,

01:37:57.780 --> 01:38:00.060
that I think have been kind of

01:38:00.060 --> 01:38:03.303
lionized and brought into formality.

01:38:04.170 --> 01:38:08.100
I don't think we're gonna be
able to explain non-conceptual

01:38:08.100 --> 01:38:09.810
judgments made by even--

01:38:09.810 --> 01:38:11.130
Even the sorts of systems,

01:38:11.130 --> 01:38:13.800
like how it is that a system
decides that you've got lung

01:38:13.800 --> 01:38:16.650
cancer or doesn't, or was that
melanoma or not and so on.

01:38:18.810 --> 01:38:21.930
I don't think we're gonna be
able to explain those things.

01:38:21.930 --> 01:38:25.770
If we hang on to the traditional notion

01:38:25.770 --> 01:38:27.720
of conceptual explanation.

01:38:27.720 --> 01:38:29.610
That doesn't mean we can't
explain 'em using words.

01:38:29.610 --> 01:38:31.260
I think this idea of
a system that can say,

01:38:31.260 --> 01:38:32.400
well look, it's kind of like this,

01:38:32.400 --> 01:38:33.780
remember this thing happened to you?

01:38:33.780 --> 01:38:35.220
Remember the feeling you had then?

01:38:35.220 --> 01:38:37.140
You know, it's this person
is kind of that state too,

01:38:37.140 --> 01:38:38.730
but they're also whatever
and blah, blah, blah.

01:38:38.730 --> 01:38:42.150
I mean, think of what a
really competent doctor can do

01:38:42.150 --> 01:38:43.743
with respect to explanation.

01:38:44.610 --> 01:38:46.713
It's often narrative in form.

01:38:48.390 --> 01:38:49.990
And I think there's something

01:38:51.120 --> 01:38:54.760
really weighty and worth pondering about

01:38:57.000 --> 01:39:01.620
the substance of narrative
with respect to explaining

01:39:01.620 --> 01:39:04.260
in ways that outscript
the model of conceptuality

01:39:04.260 --> 01:39:05.220
I'm talking about here.

01:39:05.220 --> 01:39:09.000
And the question is, how
will those be implied,

01:39:09.000 --> 01:39:13.830
not only in explaining

01:39:13.830 --> 01:39:15.240
the success of a general AI,

01:39:15.240 --> 01:39:17.523
but actually in getting
us to a general AI?

01:39:20.790 --> 01:39:22.230
So, see you and raise you one.

01:39:22.230 --> 01:39:24.153
That's my answer.

01:39:25.230 --> 01:39:26.370
- Great.

01:39:26.370 --> 01:39:28.830
Anyway, I think at this point

01:39:28.830 --> 01:39:31.260
we have kept you way too long, Brian.

01:39:31.260 --> 01:39:34.800
So, I want to thank you once
again for a wonderful talk.

01:39:34.800 --> 01:39:38.040
We look forward to the
discussion tomorrow with

